\n- [ ] **08 Notation and vocabulary, whole paper** (review change 8 and §6).\n  - One symbol per object.\n  - C for customers or columns, never both.\n  - k used consistently between sections, with the off-by-one made explicit\n    where width and stacks differ.\n  - One symbol for the border.\n  - Z for the MOSP value only.\n  - One wording for \"has a requirement\".\n  - One name for the one-dimensional-logic graph.\n  - Every term defined before use.\n  - No project-internal words in the body.\n  - Sentences over about 40 words split.\n  - **Add a notation table** at the start of the equivalences section.\n\n- [ ] **09 The conclusion.** Answer the central question in its first\n  paragraph, then:\n  - what transfers between the problems, and what does not (bands, false\n    rows);\n  - what the search episode teaches about published dominance rules;\n  - what is open (the missing Kashiwabara & Fujisawa 1979, the gap\n    `EdgeSearchMonotonicity`, the two in-flight values, the pathwidth bound\n    work).\n\n  Bring in no topic that the body does not cover.\n\n- [ ] **10 A second review.** Read the revised PDF cold, as a referee would,\n  against `paper2/review_readability.md` §1–§9.\n  - Write `paper2/review_readability_2.md`: what is fixed, what is not, and\n    any new problems.\n  - Then fix the top five new problems.\n  - Report the three-sentence reconstruction again; it should now come easily.\n\n- [ ] **11 Reserve.** The best remaining readability gap, one session.\n1:# Referee report: readability and argument structure\n13:## 0. Overall assessment\n31:## 1. Summary in three sentences, as a reader would reconstruct it\n50:## 2. The central question\n82:## 3. Section by section\n84:### Title and abstract (`main:77–107`)\n97:### Section 1, Introduction (pp. 1–3)\n118:### Section 2, \"One complex, twelve names\" (pp. 3–7)\n141:### Section 3, \"The equivalences, proved\" (pp. 8–13)\n162:### Section 4, \"An exact open-stacks search, read as a pathwidth solver\" (pp. 13–23)\n217:### Section 5, Closing (`s5`, placeholder)\n239:## 4. Figures and tables, one by one\n241:### Table 1.1, the reproduced 2002 table (p. 2, `s1:59–85`)\n249:### Table 2.1, works and citations per name (p. 4, `s2:72–81`)\n257:### Figure 2.1, bar chart of name usage (p. 5)\n265:### Figure 2.2, heat map over time (p. 6)\n273:### Figure 2.3, citation network (p. 7)\n282:### Table 3.1, the table settled (p. 9, `s3:85–113`)\n291:### Figure 3.1, the equivalence graph (p. 10)\n299:### Figure 4.1, the counterexample graph (p. 15)\n310:### Table 4.1, the dictionary (p. 19, `s4:344–361`)\n317:### Table 4.2, each rule against the literature (p. 20)\n325:### Table 4.3, cost of the repair (p. 21)\n337:### Table 4.4, the split re-certification (p. 21)\n345:### Table 4.5, certificates (p. 22)\n353:### Table 4.6, the dataset (p. 23)\n367:## 5. Theorems, lemmas and counterexamples\n397:## 6. Flow, signposting, jargon, notation\n399:### 6.1 Turns in the argument without a stated reason\n409:### 6.2 Jargon used before it is defined, or never defined\n432:### 6.3 Notation clashes\n443:### 6.4 Sentences over about 40 words (split them)\n457:### 6.5 Paragraphs with no topic sentence\n464:### 6.6 Project-internal material to remove from the main text\n479:## 7. The introduction specifically\n481:### 7.1 Evaluation of the authors' plan\n493:### 7.2 Recommended order of paragraphs and figures\n508:### 7.3 Specification of Figure 1.1 (precise enough to draw)\n568:## 8. Audience and venue fit\n606:## 9. The ten changes that would most improve readability, by impact","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
