{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017x1ka9D3eZ5aiw4Vkowj8x","type":"tool_result","content":"**Soundness.** Lemma 4.2. The search's predicate is defined over states, and\nby Lemma 4.2 it agrees with the plain question $P_k$ at every node the search\nvisits.\n\n### 4.3.2 The definite move\n\n**Published form.** \"**Theorem 1.** Suppose S ++ [q] is playable and\nclose(q, S) ≥ open(q, S), then if U′ = S ++ R is a solution, there exists a\nsolution U = S ++ [q] ++ R′\" (§3.1, PDF p. 6). Here close(q, S) =\n|{d | o(d, S) ⊆ o(q, S)}| (PDF p. 4). Chu (2011) restates it with the same\npremise, reworded for `k` stacks (\"S ++ [q] is k-playable … uses ≤ k\nstacks\") and with the same proof, as Theorem 6.3.6 (p. 142), with the same definition of\nclose (Definition 6.3.4, p. 141).\n\n**Precise form, as coded.** At a state $S$, let $q \\in P$ and let\n$$\\mathrm{close}(q, S) = |\\{d \\in C \\setminus S : o(d, S) \\subseteq o(q, S)\\}|,$$\nwhich counts $q$ itself and counts customers in $Q$. (Read literally, the\npaper's $d$ ranges over all customers. A closed $d$ has $o(d, S) = \\emptyset$\nand would always be counted, so the code's reading, $d \\notin S$, is the\nsensible one.) If $\\mathrm{open}(q, S) \\le \\mathrm{close}(q, S)$, the filter\nkeeps $L = [q]$ for the first such $q$ in index order, and nothing else runs at\nthe node (Lean: `closeCount`, `IsDefinite`). This was the code's rule until\n2026-10-01 and is `repaired_rules=False` since; section 4.6.1 gives the rule the\ncode now runs.\n\n**Claimed soundness.** $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$.\n\nWrite $X = S \\cdot q = \\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{q\\})$ throughout.\n\n**Lemma 4.4 (what the premise says).** For every $S$ and $q$,\n$\\mathrm{close}(q, S) = |X \\setminus S|$. Hence the premise\n286:which counts $q$ itself and counts customers in $Q$. (Read literally, the\n346:under the code's reading of $\\mathrm{close}$ and under the literal one, which\n348:(`chuStuckey_theorem1_false`, `chuStuckey_theorem1_false_literal`,\n351:`chuThesis_theorem636_false_literal` (`Search/ChuThesis.lean`). Fink (2012,\n377:literal sequence $S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [q, c_1, \\dots]$ need not be a solution,\n617:`chuStuckey_theorem2_false_literal`). At this node the filter still keeps customer $1$, which has\n636:extension, which is Counterexample 4.5's child. Under the literal reading of\n638:$S = \\{1\\}$, $r = 6$, $q = 12$, holds under the literal reading only.\n639:(Lean: `chuThesis_theorem638_false`, `chuThesis_theorem638_false_literal`,\n640:`chuThesis_theorem638_literal_witness`, `Search/ChuThesis.lean`; the brute","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a1c55f7b-98f8-4ea9-815f-35ebfa760af6","uuid":"733e0cd0-93d3-423d-846a-70a7c1c04cd7","timestamp":"2026-10-06T16:43:29.332Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"**Soundness.** Lemma 4.2. The search's predicate is defined over states, and\nby Lemma 4.2 it agrees with the plain question $P_k$ at every node the search\nvisits.\n\n### 4.3.2 The definite move\n\n**Published form.** \"**Theorem 1.** Suppose S ++ [q] is playable and\nclose(q, S) ≥ open(q, S), then if U′ = S ++ R is a solution, there exists a\nsolution U = S ++ [q] ++ R′\" (§3.1, PDF p. 6). Here close(q, S) =\n|{d | o(d, S) ⊆ o(q, S)}| (PDF p. 4). Chu (2011) restates it with the same\npremise, reworded for `k` stacks (\"S ++ [q] is k-playable … uses ≤ k\nstacks\") and with the same proof, as Theorem 6.3.6 (p. 142), with the same definition of\nclose (Definition 6.3.4, p. 141).\n\n**Precise form, as coded.** At a state $S$, let $q \\in P$ and let\n$$\\mathrm{close}(q, S) = |\\{d \\in C \\setminus S : o(d, S) \\subseteq o(q, S)\\}|,$$\nwhich counts $q$ itself and counts customers in $Q$. (Read literally, the\npaper's $d$ ranges over all customers. A closed $d$ has $o(d, S) = \\emptyset$\nand would always be counted, so the code's reading, $d \\notin S$, is the\nsensible one.) If $\\mathrm{open}(q, S) \\le \\mathrm{