{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfmKbkVybtMiuEEzVni8k","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014B34mAmT1NyGW4qTLtLXC9","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper2/latex/sec4_search.tex","content":"% Section 4. Sources: paper2/revised_algorithm.md (4.1-4.6), search_soundness.md,\n% prior_art_counterexample.md (prior art). Lean: lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/,\n% names in Appendix A (sec9a_appendix_lean.tex). The old 4.6-4.7 are Section 5.\n% Restructured 2026-10-06 (loop0009 item 05, review changes 4 and 5): stakes\n% first, the pathwidth reading and the dictionary in 4.1, Algorithm 4.1 in 4.2,\n% each rule as intuition -> counterexample -> repair -> test.\n% Page numbers for Chu & Stuckey (2009) are those of the preprint\n% literature/chu_stuckey_2009.pdf; for Chu (2011) the printed ones.\n\\section{An exact open-stacks search, read as a pathwidth algorithm}\n\\label{sec:search}\n\nIf MOSP is pathwidth (Theorem~\\ref{thm:mosp}), then the best exact MOSP\nalgorithm is a pathwidth algorithm, and its rules can be checked against what\nthe pathwidth literature knows. This section does that for the \\emph{customer\nsearch} of \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}, the search that Section~\\ref{sec:names}\ntraced from the open-stacks challenge. It searches over the orders in which the\ncustomers' stacks close, not over pattern sequences, and it prunes with\ndominance rules. Three of the rules are stated as theorems (their Theorems~1,\n2 and~3) and a fourth, the subset rule, in their \\S2. \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}\nplace MOSP among ``graph path-width and gate matrix layout'', citing\n\\citet{LY2002}, but they do not use pathwidth in the search. Any graph is the\nMOSP graph of the instance with one customer per vertex and one pattern per\nedge, so the search decides pathwidth for every graph.\n\nThe stakes are these. Two of the published dominance theorems, the\n\\emph{definite move} and the \\emph{better move}, are false, and so are their\nrestatements in Chu's thesis \\citep{Chu2011} and, with a stronger premise, in\n\\citet{Fink2012}. Yet no wrong answer is known: no whole instance on which the\npublished rules answer ``no'' wrongly has been found, and when every benchmark\noptimum that rested on the search was proved again with repaired rules, no\nvalue changed (Section~\\ref{sec:results}). The correct form of the definite\nmove was already known for pathwidth, as Tamaki's commitment lemma, and we\nshow that it can be tested by one small bipartite matching per candidate. The\nmain result, Theorem~\\ref{thm:main}, says informally: \\emph{with both rules\nrepaired, the search is sound; whenever it answers that $k$ stacks do not\nsuffice, they do not, and the graph has pathwidth at least $k$.} The repair\ncosts at most 2\\% more search nodes on each benchmark set\n(Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n\nSection~\\ref{sec:search-def} defines the search and shows that it decides\npathwidth, in both vocabularies. Section~\\ref{sec:algorithm} gives the\nalgorithm. Sections~\\ref{sec:definite} and~\\ref{sec:other-rules} take the\nrules one at a time, Section~\\ref{sec:theorem} proves the repaired search\nsound, and Section~\\ref{sec:layouts} places each rule in the pathwidth\nliterature.\n\n\\subsection{The search decides pathwidth}\n\\label{sec:search-def}\n\n\\citet[p.~4]{ChuStuckey2009} observe that the patterns matter only through the\nMOSP graph. So fix a graph $G=(C,E)$ whose vertices are the customers, and an\ninteger $k\\ge0$. In this section $k$ bounds the number of open stacks, which is\none more than a width: $k$ stacks suffice exactly when the pathwidth is at most\n$k-1$ (Theorem~\\ref{thm:decides}). For a customer $c$ write $N[c]$ for its\nclosed neighbourhood, the stacks that must be open when $c$'s stack closes. For\na set $T\\subseteq C$ of vertices, its \\emph{border} $\\partial T$ is the set of\nvertices outside $T$ with a neighbour in $T$. For a set $T$ of closed\ncustomers write\n\\begin{align*}\nO(T) &= \\textstyle\\bigcup_{c\\in T}N[c] && \\text{the stacks opened once $T$ has closed},\\\\\n\\fin(X) &= \\{c : N[c]\\subseteq X\\} && \\text{the customers free to close when $X$ is open},\\\\\n\\cl(T) &= \\fin(O(T)) && \\text{the \\emph{free closure} of $T$},\\\\\nb(T) &= |O(T)\\setminus T| = |\\partial T| && \\text{the stacks open after $T$ has closed},\\\\\no(c,T) &= N[c]\\setminus O(T),\\quad \\open(c,T)=|o(c,T)| && \\text{the stacks closing $c$ would newly open.}\n\\end{align*}\nThe open stacks after $T$ has closed are exactly the border of $T$, and this\none identity carries the whole translation to pathwidth.\n\nA \\emph{move} at $T$ closes one customer $c\\notin T$, at cost\n$\\cost(T,c)=|O(T\\cup\\{c\\})\\setminus T| = b(T\\cup\\{c\\})+1$, the stacks open at\nthat moment counting $c$. It is \\emph{playable} when $\\cost(T,c)\\le k$. Write\n$P_k(T)$ when some order of $C\\setminus T$ has every step playable. On entering\na node the search closes every free customer, so it stands only at\n\\emph{states}, sets with $S=\\cl(S)$. The \\emph{child} of $S$ by $c$ is\n$S\\cdot c=\\cl(S\\cup\\{c\\})$. The search's predicate $\\Sol_k(S)$ holds when\n$S=C$, or when some playable $c$ has $\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$~\\leanref{defs}. Every\nnode the search visits satisfies the invariant $b(S)\\le k$.\n\n\\paragraph{An example.} Take the graph of Figure~\\ref{fig:example}(d), with\n$k=3$. At $T=\\{a\\}$ we have $O(T)=\\{a,b\\}$, so one stack, $b$, is open:\n$b(T)=1$ and $\\partial T=\\{b\\}$. No other customer is free, since $N[b]$\ncontains $c$ and $d$, so $T$ is a state. Closing $b$ newly opens\n$o(b,T)=\\{c,d\\}$ and costs $\\cost(T,b)=3$, the stacks $b$, $c$ and $d$; the\nmove is playable. At $\\{a,b,c\\}$ every neighbour of $d$ is open, so $d$ is\nfree, and the child $\\{a,b\\}\\cdot c$ is $\\{a,b,c,d\\}$. The closing order\n$a,b,c,e$ (with $d$ and $f$ closed free) costs $2,3,3,2$, the profile of\norder~A in Figure~\\ref{fig:example}(a).\n\n\\begin{lemma}[free moves never hurt]\\label{lem:F}\nIf $b(T)\\le k$, then $P_k(T)\\iff\\Sol_k(\\cl(T))$~\\leanref{F}.\n\\end{lemma}\nClosing a customer whose neighbourhood is already open opens nothing, never\nmakes a later step dearer, and leaves one fewer stack open. The hypothesis is\nneeded: closing the free customers one by one costs $b(T)$ at each step.\n\n\\begin{theorem}[the search decides pathwidth]\\label{thm:decides}\nFor nonempty $C$, $\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)\\iff\\pw(G)+1\\le k$. On the MOSP graph of an\ninstance $M$ with at least one 1, $\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)\\iff Z(M)\\le k$~\\leanref{decides}.\n\\end{theorem}\n\\begin{proof}\nFor a closing order $c_1,\\dots,c_n$ with prefixes $S_i$, the $i$-th step costs\n$1+|\\partial S_i|$. Reverse the order to get a layout. Its first $n-i$\nvertices are $C\\setminus S_i$, and those with a neighbour among the remaining\nones are exactly $\\partial S_i$. So the cost of the closing order is the vertex\nseparation of the reversed layout plus one, and Lemma~\\ref{lem:F} at the root\nand Theorem~\\ref{thm:vs} finish the proof.\n\\end{proof}\n\nSo a refutation, $\\neg\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)$, may only mean $\\pw(G)>k-1$. The rest\nof the section asks whether the pruning rules preserve that meaning.\n\n\\paragraph{In pathwidth language.} The exact pathwidth literature writes\n$N(T)$ for the border and $d(T)$ for its size\n\\citep[p.~390]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014}; we keep $\\partial T$ and $b(T)$. A\nvertex sequence is \\emph{$w$-feasible} when every prefix has border at most\n$w$, and $\\vs(G)$ is the least $w$ for which some permutation is $w$-feasible.\nTable~\\ref{tab:dictionary} translates each notion of the search into this\nvocabulary. Read it row by row: every ingredient of the customer search is a\nknown ingredient of an exact pathwidth algorithm, and the last two rows are\nthe rules of Section~\\ref{sec:algorithm}.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{The customer search is, ingredient by ingredient, an exact pathwidth\nalgorithm: each notion of the search beside its name in the pathwidth\nliterature. The memo and the definite move are described in\nSection~\\ref{sec:algorithm}.}\n\\label{tab:dictionary}\n\\begin{tabularx}{\\linewidth}{@{}>{\\raggedright}p{5.2cm}>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X@{}}\n\\toprule\nCustomer search & Layout \\\\\n\\midrule\nclosing order from $\\emptyset$ & vertex sequence, read forwards \\\\\nopen stacks $b(T)$ & border size $|\\partial T|$ \\\\\n$\\cost(T,c)$ & $|\\partial(T\\cup\\{c\\})|+1$ \\\\\n$\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)$ & $\\vs(G)\\le k-1$ \\\\\nfree closure $\\cl(T)$ & full set of $T$ \\citep[p.~328]{SuchanVillanger2009} \\\\\nmemo & failure table \\\\\ndefinite move, repaired & commitment to the child \\\\\n\\bottomrule\\end{tabularx}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{The algorithm}\n\\label{sec:algorithm}\n\nAlgorithm~\\ref{alg:search} gives the customer search. It is a depth-first\nsearch over states. At each node it closes the free customers, consults a\ntable of states already refuted, keeps the playable candidates, filters them\nwith the dominance rules and recurses on the survivors.\n\n\\begin{algorithm}[t]\n\\caption{The customer search \\citep{ChuStuckey2009}. It decides $\\Sol_k(S)$\nfor a graph $G=(C,E)$ and a stack bound $k$, and is called as\n$\\textsc{Search}(\\emptyset,\\emptyset)$. $Q$ is the set of old moves the node\ninherits.}\n\\label{alg:search}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}r@{\\quad}l@{\\qquad}l@{}}\n1 & $\\textsc{Search}(S,Q)$: & \\\\\n2 & \\quad $S\\gets\\cl(S)$ & \\textcolor{gray}{free moves (Lemma~\\ref{lem:F})} \\\\\n3 & \\quad \\textbf{if} $S=C$ \\textbf{then return} true & \\\\\n4 & \\quad \\textbf{if} $S$ is in the memo \\textbf{then return} false & \\textcolor{gray}{memo} \\\\\n5 & \\quad $P\\gets$ the $c\\notin S\\cup Q$ with $\\cost(S,c)\\le k$, in index order & \\textcolor{gray}{cost cut} \\\\\n6 & \\quad $L\\gets$ filter of $P$: definite move, then subset rule, then better move & \\textcolor{gray}{Sections~\\ref{sec:definite}--\\ref{sec:other-rules}} \\\\\n7 & \\quad $\\mathit{seen}\\gets Q\\setminus S$ & \\\\\n8 & \\quad \\textbf{for each} $c\\in L$: & \\\\\n9 & \\quad\\quad $Q'\\gets\\{q\\in\\mathit{seen} : \\cost(S\\cup\\{q\\},c)\\le k\\}$ & \\textcolor{gray}{old moves} \\\\\n10 & \\quad\\quad \\textbf{if} $\\textsc{Search}(S\\cup\\{c\\},Q')$ \\textbf{then return} true & \\\\\n11 & \\quad\\quad $\\mathit{seen}\\gets\\mathit{seen}\\cup\\{c\\}$ & \\\\\n12 & \\quad add $S$ to the memo & \\\\\n13 & \\quad \\textbf{return} false & \\\\\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{algorithm}\n\nLine~2 is safe by Lemma~\\ref{lem:F}, and the cost cut of line~5 is exact,\nsince an unplayable move is not a move of $\\Sol_k$. The rest is pruning, and\neach piece has a one-sentence intuition.\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\emph{The memo} (lines~4 and~12): whether a state can be finished does\n  not depend on how it was reached, so a state refuted once is refused when\n  met again.\n\\item \\emph{Old moves} (lines~7--11): a sibling whose subtree failed stays\n  failed further down, as long as closing it first would have kept each later\n  step within the bound.\n\\item \\emph{The definite move}: if closing $q$ now leaves no more stacks open\n  than before, it seems safe to close $q$ at once and try nothing else.\n\\item \\emph{The subset rule}: if $d$ newly opens a subset of what $r$ newly\n  opens, then $d$ is at least as good a move as $r$.\n\\item \\emph{The better move}: if $q$ would be a definite move right after $r$,\n  then closing $q$ before $r$ is at least as good as closing $r$ first.\n\\end{itemize}\nEach of the three filtering rules discards a move $r$ by citing a\n\\emph{covering}, a kept move $c$ with\n$\\Sol_k(S\\cdot r)\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$. The filter runs the definite\nmove, then the subset rule, then the better move, each once. If the definite\nmove fires, it keeps its customer alone.\n\nThis loop is the decision procedure of \\citet[Algorithm~1,\np.~393]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014}, which asks whether a vertex set extends to\na $w$-feasible permutation: replace the set by a committed extension, answer\nfrom a failure table or at the full vertex set, recurse on each feasible\none-vertex extension, and record failures. The customer search is that loop\nwith $w=k-1$. The free closure and the definite move play the part of the\ncommitment, the memo is the failure table, and the cost cut is the feasibility\ntest. \\citet[\\S3.2]{CoudertMazauricNisse2014} has the same three parts in a\nbranch and bound. Neither line cites the other in the papers we hold:\n\\citet{ChuStuckey2009} predates the commitments and both 2014 papers, and none\nof the pathwidth papers cites it. This is the isolation measured in\nTable~\\ref{tab:communities}, at the level of one algorithm.\n\n\\subsection{The definite move is false as published, and its repair is a matching}\n\\label{sec:definite}\n\nThe definite move is the strongest of the rules, since when it fires it\ndiscards every other candidate. Its premise looks like a safe accounting:\nclosing $q$ opens some new stacks and lets some customers close, and if it\nlets at least as many close as it opens, nothing seems lost by closing it\nfirst. \\citet[\\S3.1, p.~6]{ChuStuckey2009} state:\n\\begin{quote}\n\\textbf{Theorem 1.} Suppose S ++ [q] is playable and close(q, S) $\\ge$\nopen(q, S), then if U$'$ = S ++ R is a solution, there exists a solution\nU = S ++ [q] ++ R$'$.\n\\end{quote}\nHere ++ is concatenation of closing sequences, a \\emph{solution} is a complete\nclosing order with every step playable, and $\\close(q,S)$ counts the customers\n$d$ with $o(d,S)\\subseteq o(q,S)$ (p.~4). The rule keeps $q$ alone. We read $d$\nas ranging over customers not yet closed, as our implementation does; read\nliterally, closed customers count too, which weakens the premise, and both\nreadings are refuted below. \\citet[Thm~6.3.6, p.~142]{Chu2011} restates the\ntheorem with the same premise and proof, reworded for $k$ stacks. Write\n$X=S\\cdot q$. The first lemma confirms that the premise says what the\nintuition says.\n\n\\begin{lemma}[what the premise says]\\label{lem:premise}\n$\\close(q,S)=|X\\setminus S|$. Hence the premise holds iff $b(X)\\le b(S)$: the\nchild has no more open stacks than the parent~\\leanref{premise}.\n\\end{lemma}\n\nThe premise compares only the two ends, $S$ and $X$. The rule fails because a\nset between them can be cheaper still. Figure~\\ref{fig:cex} shows the graph on\nwhich it fails.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.85\\linewidth]{definite_counterexample.pdf}\n\\caption{The graph of Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} at the state where the\ndefinite move fails. Customer 2 is closed; 1, 3 and 4 are open. Moving 0 opens\nthe new stacks 0, 7 and 11 and closes 0, 3 and 4, so open = close = 3 and the\nrule keeps 0 alone. But 3 and 4 have the single new stack 0 between them:\nclosing them first gains two stacks for the price of one, which a solution\nstarting with 0 cannot match.}\n\\label{fig:cex}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{counterexample}\\label{cex:definite}\nLet $G$ be the graph of Figure~\\ref{fig:cex}, on customers $0,\\dots,13$ with\nthe 26 edges\n\\begin{gather*}\n0\\text{--}3,\\ 0\\text{--}4,\\ 0\\text{--}7,\\ 0\\text{--}11,\\ 1\\text{--}2,\\ 1\\text{--}5,\\ 1\\text{--}6,\\ 2\\text{--}3,\\ 2\\text{--}4,\\ 5\\text{--}9,\\ 5\\text{--}11,\\ 5\\text{--}12,\\ 6\\text{--}8,\\\\\n6\\text{--}13,\\ 7\\text{--}9,\\ 7\\text{--}10,\\ 7\\text{--}13,\\ 8\\text{--}9,\\ 8\\text{--}10,\\ 8\\text{--}11,\\ 9\\text{--}10,\\ 9\\text{--}11,\\ 10\\text{--}11,\\ 10\\text{--}12,\\ 10\\text{--}13,\\ 12\\text{--}13,\n\\end{gather*}\nwith $S=\\{2\\}$, $k=6$ and $q=0$. Then $O(S)=\\{1,2,3,4\\}$,\n$o(0,S)=\\{0,7,11\\}$ and $\\cost(S,0)=6$, so $0$ is playable. The customers with\n$o(d,S)\\subseteq\\{0,7,11\\}$ are $0$, $3$ and $4$, so $\\close(0,S)=3\\ge\\open(0,S)$,\nand $0$ comes first in index order, so the rule keeps only $0$. A solution\nexists from $S$: the order\n\\[1,3,4,6,12,13,0,5,7,8,9,10,11\\]\ncosts at most 6. None exists from the child $X=\\{0,2,3,4\\}$: only seven sets\nare reachable from $X$ by moves of cost at most 6, and none is $C$~\\leanref{cex}.\n\\end{counterexample}\n\nThe published implication, as a universal statement, is refuted in Lean under\nboth readings of $\\close$, and so is the thesis's Theorem~6.3.6~\\leanref{thm1false}.\n\\citet[pp.~27--28]{Fink2012} restates the rule with a stronger premise,\ncounting only dominated customers of smaller index than $q$. The graph above\nrefutes it under no labelling, but a third twin of 3 and 4 gives a\n15-customer graph that does~\\leanref{fink}. The published proof moves $q$ to\nthe front of a solution and counts, after each prefix, the stacks $q$ opens\nagainst the customers it lets close. But customers that the solution has\nalready closed no longer count as closed by $q$, while the stacks $q$ opens may\nstill be new. Appendix~\\ref{app:whyfails} gives the full analysis, and\nAppendix~\\ref{app:minimality} the search for smaller counterexamples.\n\n\\paragraph{The repair.} The fix is to require the comparison at every set\nbetween the two ends, not only at $S$. Call $q$ \\emph{hereditarily definite} at\n$S$ when $b(X)\\le b(B)$ for every $B$ with $S\\subseteq B\\subseteq X$ and\n$q\\notin B$. By Lemma~\\ref{lem:premise}, the published premise is the case\n$B=S$. In the counterexample the repair fails at $B=\\{2,3,4\\}$, where\n$b(B)=2<3=b(X)$~\\leanref{hered}. The proof that the repair is sound needs one\ninequality.\n\n\\begin{lemma}[open stacks are submodular]\\label{lem:submod}\nFor all $A,B\\subseteq C$, $b(A\\cup B)+b(A\\cap B)\\le b(A)+b(B)$~\\leanref{submod}.\n\\end{lemma}\n\\begin{proof}\n$b(T)=|O(T)|-|T|$, $O(A\\cup B)=O(A)\\cup O(B)$ and $O(A\\cap B)\\subseteq\nO(A)\\cap O(B)$; the customers themselves balance exactly.\n\\end{proof}\n\n\\begin{theorem}[the repaired definite move is sound]\\label{thm:repaired}\nIf $q\\notin S$ is hereditarily definite at $S$, then $P_k(S)\\Rightarrow P_k(X)$.\nAt a state with the invariant, $\\Sol_k(S)\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$~\\leanref{repaired}.\n\\end{theorem}\n\\begin{proof}\nTake an order from $S$ of cost at most $k$ with prefixes $T_i$, in which $q$ is\nthe $j$-th step, and follow it from $X$, skipping customers already in $X$. A\nstep $i<j$ closes $c_i$ at $T_{i-1}\\cup X$. We have\n$S\\subseteq T_i\\cap X\\subseteq X$ and $q\\notin T_i$, so the hypothesis gives\n$b(X)\\le b(T_i\\cap X)$, and Lemma~\\ref{lem:submod} gives\n$b(T_i\\cup X)\\le b(T_i)$: the step costs no more than the original. After step\n$j-1$ the closed set contains $T_j$ with the same opened set, and the rest of\nthe order applies. The search's form follows by Lemma~\\ref{lem:F}.\n\\end{proof}\n\nThis theorem is not new. The hereditary premise is the commitment condition of\nTamaki, and the proof is the proof of the commitment lemma,\n\\citet[Lemma~1, p.~142]{Kitsunai2016}; Section~\\ref{sec:layouts} makes the\ncorrespondence exact.\n\n\\paragraph{The matching test.} The repaired rule seems to require checking\nexponentially many intermediate sets. It does not, and this cheap test is the\nalgorithmic contribution of the section; we did not find it in the papers we\nhold. In words: the rule is safe exactly when all but one of the new stacks\ncan be paired with a different freed customer who needed it. In the\ncounterexample, closing 0 opens three new stacks and frees 3 and 4, both of\nwhom needed only stack 0: one pairing where two are required.\n\n\\begin{theorem}[the repair is a matching condition]\\label{thm:matching}\nLet $Y=X\\setminus(S\\cup\\{q\\})$, and join each $d\\in Y$ to the stacks of\n$o(d,S)\\subseteq o(q,S)$. Then $q$ is hereditarily definite at $S$ iff this\nbipartite graph has a matching with at least $\\open(q,S)-1$ edges~\\leanref{matching}.\n\\end{theorem}\n\\begin{proof}\nThe sets of the repair are $B=S\\cup D$, $D\\subseteq Y$, and\n$b(X)\\le b(S\\cup D)$ iff $|D|-|o(D)|\\le\\delta$ with\n$\\delta=|Y|+1-\\open(q,S)$, where $o(D)$ is the union of the $o(d,S)$. By the\ndeficiency form of Hall's theorem the largest matching has size\n$|Y|-\\max_D(|D|-|o(D)|)$, which is at least $|Y|-\\delta=\\open(q,S)-1$ exactly\nwhen the repair holds.\n\\end{proof}\n\nThe bipartite graph has at most $\\close-1$ customers on one side and $\\open$\nstacks on the other, and the matching can stop at $\\open-1$ edges. Every $q$\nwith $\\open(q,S)\\le1$ passes at once, so the published rule is right\nthere~\\leanref{open1}.\n\n\\subsection{The other rules}\n\\label{sec:other-rules}\n\nEach of the remaining rules is quoted, proved sound in the form used, and\nshown to need the test or tie-break it carries.\n\n\\paragraph{The subset rule.} ``If $o(c_i,S)\\subseteq o(c_j,S)$ and $i<j$ \\dots\\\nmove $j$ can be removed'' \\citep[\\S2, p.~5]{ChuStuckey2009}. As implemented,\n$d$ dominates $r$ when $o(d,S)\\subseteq o(r,S)$ and the inclusion is strict or\n$d<r$. Every dominated playable $r$ is dropped, with a fallback that keeps $P$\nif all would go. The covering is sound for every set $S$: if\n$o(d,S)\\subseteq o(r,S)$ and $r$ is playable, then $d$ is playable and\n$\\Sol_k(S\\cdot r)\\Rightarrow \\Sol_k(S\\cdot d)$, because $d$ is free after $r$\nand closing $d$ then $r$ reaches the same state at no greater cost. With the\nindex tie-break, domination is a strict order, so an undominated move with a\nsolution survives, and the fallback never fires when a solution\nexists~\\leanref{subset}. The tie-break is necessary: without it, two customers\nwith equal new stacks dominate each other and both go, which loses a solution\non a 7-customer graph~\\leanref{tiebreak}.\n\n\\paragraph{The better move.} \\citet[\\S3.2, p.~6]{ChuStuckey2009}: ``Suppose\nS ++ [q] and S ++ [r, q] are playable and close(q, S $\\cup$ \\{r\\}) $\\ge$\nopen(q, S $\\cup$ \\{r\\}) then if U$'$ = S ++ [r] ++ R is a solution there exists a\nsolution U = S ++ [q] ++ R$'$.'' Its premise is the definite move's premise for\n$q$ at the child $\\cl(S\\cup\\{r\\})$, together with playability of $r$ then $q$,\nand its conclusion swaps $q$ and $r$~\\leanref{isbetter}. The swap is sound,\nbecause both orders end at the same state and neither step overflows. The\nappeal to the definite move is not.\n\n\\emph{Counterexample.} On the graph of Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} at the\nroot with $k=6$, the definite move does not fire, customers 0 and 2 both\nsurvive the subset rule, and the premise holds for $r=2$, $q=0$, so the rule\ndrops 2 citing 0. But $\\{2\\}$ has a solution and $\\{0\\}$ has\nnone~\\leanref{bettercex}. At that node another kept customer still has a\nsolution, so this is a false link and not a lost node; no node where the\nbetter move loses the last solution has been found. \\citet[Thm~6.3.8,\np.~143]{Chu2011} states the better move with a different premise,\n$\\close(q,S)\\ge\\open(q,S\\cup\\{r\\})$, and proves it through his Theorem~6.3.6.\nThe same graph refutes it at $S=\\{2\\}$, $r=3$, $q=0$~\\leanref{thm638}.\n\n\\emph{Repair and test.} Cite $q$ for $r$ only if $q$ is hereditarily definite\nat $\\cl(S\\cup\\{r\\})$, which is one matching at the child\n(Theorem~\\ref{thm:matching}); then the swap argument goes\nthrough~\\leanref{repbetter}.\n\n\\emph{Two requirements.} The close count in the premise must not include the\ncustomers that $r$ finishes on its own; counting them makes the rule's own\nconclusion false on a 12-customer graph. And the rules must run in the order\ndefinite, subset, better, each citing only customers still standing: if the\nbetter move runs first, the subset rule can cite a customer the better move\nhas already dropped, and an 8-customer graph loses a node through a cycle of\nlinks that are each true~\\leanref{bugs}. The second is an instance of the\ngeneral observation of \\citet[Prop.~3, Example~6]{Beck2025} that mutually\ndominating transitions must be tie-broken.\n\n\\paragraph{The old move.} \\citet[Thm~3, p.~7]{ChuStuckey2009} let a child\ninherit a sibling $q$ whose subtree failed, provided that inserting $q$ before\neach later step keeps that step playable (line~9 of\nAlgorithm~\\ref{alg:search}). The rule is sound as implemented: if\n$\\cost(S\\cup\\{q\\},c)\\le k$, then\n$\\Sol_k(\\cl(\\{q\\}\\cup S\\cdot c))\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$, so a refuted\nsibling stays refuted along any path whose steps pass the test~\\leanref{oldmove}.\nThe test is necessary; the smallest graph that shows it is the path on five\nvertices~\\leanref{reinsert}.\n\n\\paragraph{The memo.} Whether a state with $b(S)\\le k$ has a solution depends\non the state alone, not on the path that reached it~\\leanref{memo}. One\nconcern is that the memo and the old move might not combine: a refutation\nobtained with inherited old moves seems to depend on the ancestors, and one\nimplementation of the search declines to run both for that reason. A node\ntaken on its own, with an old move that is not in fact refuted, can indeed\nrecord a solvable state~\\leanref{fakeold}. Theorem~\\ref{thm:runs}\n(Appendix~\\ref{app:machinery}) shows that this never happens in a real run,\nbecause every old move is a refutation completed earlier in the same run.\n\n\\subsection{The repaired search is sound}\n\\label{sec:theorem}\n\n\\paragraph{What a rule must satisfy.} At a state $S$ with old moves $Q$, the\ncost cut keeps the playable candidates $P$ and the filter keeps a sublist\n$L\\subseteq P$. The filter is \\emph{node-sound} at $(S,Q)$ if, whenever every\n$q\\in Q$ has $\\neg\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$, $\\Sol_k(S)$ implies $\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$ for\nsome $c\\in L$~\\leanref{nodesound}. In terms of coverings, a node is sound when\ntwo things hold. First, every link a rule cites is a true covering. Second,\nevery chain of links from a discarded candidate ends in $L$ or in $Q$. The\nsecond condition can fail through a cycle even when every link is true, which\nis why the order of the rules matters. Proposition~\\ref{prop:filter} shows that\nthe repaired filter is node-sound, and Theorem~\\ref{thm:runs} that node\nsoundness, the memo and the old move together give sound runs; both are in\nAppendix~\\ref{app:machinery}. Together they give the main theorem.\n\n\\begin{theorem}[the revised search is sound]\\label{thm:main}\nRun the customer search with free moves, the memo, old moves and the filter\ndefinite $\\to$ subset $\\to$ better, the definite move firing only on a\nhereditarily definite customer and the better move citing only under the\nrepaired premise. If it refutes $k$ from the root, then no closing order costs\nat most $k$, $\\pw(G)>k-1$, and on the MOSP graph of an instance $M$ with at\nleast one 1, $Z(M)>k$~\\leanref{main}.\n\\end{theorem}\n\nIn words: each rule throws away only moves that a kept move is proved to be at\nleast as good as, and the memo and the old moves remember only failures that\nreally happened earlier in the same search. So when the search says ``no'',\nno closing order fits in $k$ stacks.\n\n\\paragraph{What is proved and what is tested.} The theorem is about the search\nas specified; that our C and Python implementations run it is checked, not\nproved. Three checks link them:\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item a transcription of the Lean filter agreed with the implemented filter on\n  16,244,090 node checks over graphs on 1 to 17 vertices;\n\\item the matching agreed with the hereditary premise on 36,939,226 candidates;\n\\item a search written from the theorems agreed with both implementations and\n  an exact oracle on 17,431,232 runs over 52,592 graphs on 1 to 17 vertices.\n\\end{itemize}\nSection~\\ref{sec:results} adds a certificate checker for individual\nrefutations.\n\n\\paragraph{The published rules.} Under the published rules the filter is not\nnode-sound~\\leanref{notsound}. In a check of 570,206 runs with memo and old\nmove, it lost the last solution at a node in 58 runs, all on graphs built\naround Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} and all at $k$ equal to the optimum.\nEvery one found a solution through another branch and answered correctly. No\nwhole instance on which the published rules answer \\emph{unsat} wrongly is\nknown.\n\n\\subsection{The rules in pathwidth language}\n\\label{sec:layouts}\n\nThe definite move has an exact counterpart in the pathwidth literature, and\nthis subsection makes the correspondence precise.\n\\citet[p.~142]{Kitsunai2016}, stating Tamaki's notion, call an extension $\\tau$\nof a vertex sequence $\\sigma$ \\emph{committable} when\n$|\\partial X'|\\ge|\\partial V(\\tau)|$ for every $X'$ with\n$V(\\sigma)\\subseteq X'\\subseteq V(\\tau)$. Their Lemma~1 says that if $\\sigma$\nextends to a feasible permutation, so does $\\tau$.\n\n\\begin{lemma}\\label{lem:endpoint}\nFor $q\\notin S$ and $X=\\cl(S\\cup\\{q\\})$: the published premise holds iff\n$b(X)\\le b(S)$, the commitment condition at the single set $X'=S$; and the\nrepaired premise holds iff $X$ is a commitment from $S$, the condition at every\n$X'$ between them~\\leanref{endpoint}.\n\\end{lemma}\n\nSo Theorem~\\ref{thm:repaired} is the commitment lemma at the child, and the\npublished definite move is a commitment whose interior condition was dropped.\nIn Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} the endpoint test passes with\n$b(X)=3=b(S)$, while the interior set $\\{2,3,4\\}$ has border\n2~\\leanref{cexnotcommit}. The published premise is not wasted: by\n\\citet[Lemma~10, Cor.~2, pp.~148--149]{Kitsunai2016} it guarantees a\ncommitment to the least-border set between $S$ and $X$, which need not contain\n$q$~\\leanref{existscommit}. In the counterexample that set is\n$\\{2,3,4\\}$~\\leanref{commit234}. Committing there when the matching fails is a\nsecond repair that gives up no pruning; we have not built it.\n\nA commitment of depth one, $S$ to $S\\cup\\{q\\}$, holds iff\n$\\open(q,S)\\le1$~\\leanref{depth1}. The greedy step of\n\\citet[Lemma~3, p.~49]{CoudertMazauricNisse2014} is exactly that\ncondition~\\leanref{greedy}, and the push rule of\n\\citet[Rule~1, p.~330]{SuchanVillanger2009} is a case of it. So every published\npathwidth rule of this kind lies in the region $\\open\\le1$, where the published\ndefinite move is right; \\citet[p.~388, Table~2]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014}\nreport depth-one commitments as ``extremely effective''. Chu and Stuckey's\ndefinite move reaches into $\\open\\ge2$, and that is where it fails. For the\ncost of the test, \\citet[Cor.~2]{Kitsunai2016} find the least border between\nthe ends of a given extension by a minimum $s$--$t$ separator;\nTheorem~\\ref{thm:matching} is that computation for the single target $X$, where\nit becomes a bipartite matching.\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:known} sums up, and its rows are grouped by what they say. The\npublished definite and better moves are false. The free move and the repaired\ndefinite move are known pathwidth results. The subset rule, the repaired\nbetter move and the old move have no counterpart we found, and are proved\nhere, as is the matching test. The comparison is limited to the pathwidth\npapers we hold\n\\citep{SuchanVillanger2009,Bodlaender2012,CoudertMazauricNisse2014,CoudertMazauricNisse2016,KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014,Kitsunai2016};\n``none found'' is not a claim of priority.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{Two published rules are false, two repaired or reused rules are known\npathwidth results, and the rest, with the matching test, are proved here: each\nrule of the customer search against the exact-pathwidth literature.}\n\\label{tab:known}\n\\begin{tabularx}{\\linewidth}{@{}>{\\raggedright}p{3.2cm}>{\\raggedright}X>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}p{4.1cm}@{}}\n\\toprule\nRule & Published counterpart & Status \\\\\n\\midrule\ndefinite move, published & -- & false (Cex.~\\ref{cex:definite}) \\\\\nbetter move, published & none found & false \\\\\n\\midrule\nfree move & full sets \\citep{SuchanVillanger2009}; \\citet[Prop.~3]{Kitsunai2016} & known \\\\\ndefinite move, $\\open\\le1$ & depth-one commitments; greedy step; push rule & known \\\\\ndefinite move, repaired & commitment lemma \\citep[Lemma~1]{Kitsunai2016} & known; matching test (Thm~\\ref{thm:matching}) not found \\\\\nmemo & subset DP \\citep{Bodlaender2012}; prefix and failure tables & known; with the old move, proved here (Thm~\\ref{thm:runs}) \\\\\n\\midrule\nsubset rule & none found & proved here \\\\\nbetter move, repaired & none found & proved here \\\\\nold move & none found & proved here \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabularx}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Prior reports.} We found no earlier report of the error, in 56 of\nthe 141 works that cite the customer search, read in full\n(Appendix~\\ref{app:prior}). The pathwidth literature has the correct form of\nthe rule, as a commitment, and none of the pathwidth papers we hold mentions\nMOSP.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1660,"cache_read_input_tokens":138487,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1660,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a1c55f7b-98f8-4ea9-815f-35ebfa760af6","uuid":"5954fb6f-b4f3-430c-be2c-9990aecf1939","timestamp":"2026-10-06T16:47:10.910Z","request_id":"req_011CfmKbjafnC8b7CyT7kf3M","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_014B34mAmT1NyGW4qTLtLXC9":{"file_path":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper2/latex/sec4_search.tex","content":"% Section 4. Sources: paper2/revised_algorithm.md (4.1-4.6), search_soundness.md,\n% prior_art_counterexample.md (prior art). Lean: lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/,\n% names in Appendix A (sec9a_appendix_lean.tex). The old 4.6-4.7 are Section 5.\n% Restructured 2026-10-06 (loop0009 item 05, review changes 4 and 5): stakes\n% first, the pathwidth reading and the dictionary in 4.1, Algorithm 4.1 in 4.2,\n% each rule as intuition -> counterexample -> repair -> test.\n% Page numbers for Chu & Stuckey (2009) are those of the preprint\n% literature/chu_stuckey_2009.pdf; for Chu (2011) the printed ones.\n\\section{An exact open-stacks search, read as a pathwidth algorithm}\n\\label{sec:search}\n\nIf MOSP is pathwidth (Theorem~\\ref{thm:mosp}), then the best exact MOSP\nalgorithm is a pathwidth algorithm, and its rules can be checked against what\nthe pathwidth literature knows. This section does that for the \\emph{customer\nsearch} of \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}, the search that Section~\\ref{sec:names}\ntraced from the open-stacks challenge. It searches over the orders in which the\ncustomers' stacks close, not over pattern sequences, and it prunes with\ndominance rules. Three of the rules are stated as theorems (their Theorems~1,\n2 and~3) and a fourth, the subset rule, in their \\S2. \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}\nplace MOSP among ``graph path-width and gate matrix layout'', citing\n\\citet{LY2002}, but they do not use pathwidth in the search. Any graph is the\nMOSP graph of the instance with one customer per vertex and one pattern per\nedge, so the search decides pathwidth for every graph.\n\nThe stakes are these. Two of the published dominance theorems, the\n\\emph{definite move} and the \\emph{better move}, are false, and so are their\nrestatements in Chu's thesis \\citep{Chu2011} and, with a stronger premise, in\n\\citet{Fink2012}. Yet no wrong answer is known: no whole instance on which the\npublished rules answer ``no'' wrongly has been found, and when every benchmark\noptimum that rested on the search was proved again with repaired rules, no\nvalue changed (Section~\\ref{sec:results}). The correct form of the definite\nmove was already known for pathwidth, as Tamaki's commitment lemma, and we\nshow that it can be tested by one small bipartite matching per candidate. The\nmain result, Theorem~\\ref{thm:main}, says informally: \\emph{with both rules\nrepaired, the search is sound; whenever it answers that $k$ stacks do not\nsuffice, they do not, and the graph has pathwidth at least $k$.} The repair\ncosts at most 2\\% more search nodes on each benchmark set\n(Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n\nSection~\\ref{sec:search-def} defines the search and shows that it decides\npathwidth, in both vocabularies. Section~\\ref{sec:algorithm} gives the\nalgorithm. Sections~\\ref{sec:definite} and~\\ref{sec:other-rules} take the\nrules one at a time, Section~\\ref{sec:theorem} proves the repaired search\nsound, and Section~\\ref{sec:layouts} places each rule in the pathwidth\nliterature.\n\n\\subsection{The search decides pathwidth}\n\\label{sec:search-def}\n\n\\citet[p.~4]{ChuStuckey2009} observe that the patterns matter only through the\nMOSP graph. So fix a graph $G=(C,E)$ whose vertices are the customers, and an\ninteger $k\\ge0$. In this section $k$ bounds the number of open stacks, which is\none more than a width: $k$ stacks suffice exactly when the pathwidth is at most\n$k-1$ (Theorem~\\ref{thm:decides}). For a customer $c$ write $N[c]$ for its\nclosed neighbourhood, the stacks that must be open when $c$'s stack closes. For\na set $T\\subseteq C$ of vertices, its \\emph{border} $\\partial T$ is the set of\nvertices outside $T$ with a neighbour in $T$. For a set $T$ of closed\ncustomers write\n\\begin{align*}\nO(T) &= \\textstyle\\bigcup_{c\\in T}N[c] && \\text{the stacks opened once $T$ has closed},\\\\\n\\fin(X) &= \\{c : N[c]\\subseteq X\\} && \\text{the customers free to close when $X$ is open},\\\\\n\\cl(T) &= \\fin(O(T)) && \\text{the \\emph{free closure} of $T$},\\\\\nb(T) &= |O(T)\\setminus T| = |\\partial T| && \\text{the stacks open after $T$ has closed},\\\\\no(c,T) &= N[c]\\setminus O(T),\\quad \\open(c,T)=|o(c,T)| && \\text{the stacks closing $c$ would newly open.}\n\\end{align*}\nThe open stacks after $T$ has closed are exactly the border of $T$, and this\none identity carries the whole translation to pathwidth.\n\nA \\emph{move} at $T$ closes one customer $c\\notin T$, at cost\n$\\cost(T,c)=|O(T\\cup\\{c\\})\\setminus T| = b(T\\cup\\{c\\})+1$, the stacks open at\nthat moment counting $c$. It is \\emph{playable} when $\\cost(T,c)\\le k$. Write\n$P_k(T)$ when some order of $C\\setminus T$ has every step playable. On entering\na node the search closes every free customer, so it stands only at\n\\emph{states}, sets with $S=\\cl(S)$. The \\emph{child} of $S$ by $c$ is\n$S\\cdot c=\\cl(S\\cup\\{c\\})$. The search's predicate $\\Sol_k(S)$ holds when\n$S=C$, or when some playable $c$ has $\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$~\\leanref{defs}. Every\nnode the search visits satisfies the invariant $b(S)\\le k$.\n\n\\paragraph{An example.} Take the graph of Figure~\\ref{fig:example}(d), with\n$k=3$. At $T=\\{a\\}$ we have $O(T)=\\{a,b\\}$, so one stack, $b$, is open:\n$b(T)=1$ and $\\partial T=\\{b\\}$. No other customer is free, since $N[b]$\ncontains $c$ and $d$, so $T$ is a state. Closing $b$ newly opens\n$o(b,T)=\\{c,d\\}$ and costs $\\cost(T,b)=3$, the stacks $b$, $c$ and $d$; the\nmove is playable. At $\\{a,b,c\\}$ every neighbour of $d$ is open, so $d$ is\nfree, and the child $\\{a,b\\}\\cdot c$ is $\\{a,b,c,d\\}$. The closing order\n$a,b,c,e$ (with $d$ and $f$ closed free) costs $2,3,3,2$, the profile of\norder~A in Figure~\\ref{fig:example}(a).\n\n\\begin{lemma}[free moves never hurt]\\label{lem:F}\nIf $b(T)\\le k$, then $P_k(T)\\iff\\Sol_k(\\cl(T))$~\\leanref{F}.\n\\end{lemma}\nClosing a customer whose neighbourhood is already open opens nothing, never\nmakes a later step dearer, and leaves one fewer stack open. The hypothesis is\nneeded: closing the free customers one by one costs $b(T)$ at each step.\n\n\\begin{theorem}[the search decides pathwidth]\\label{thm:decides}\nFor nonempty $C$, $\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)\\iff\\pw(G)+1\\le k$. On the MOSP graph of an\ninstance $M$ with at least one 1, $\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)\\iff Z(M)\\le k$~\\leanref{decides}.\n\\end{theorem}\n\\begin{proof}\nFor a closing order $c_1,\\dots,c_n$ with prefixes $S_i$, the $i$-th step costs\n$1+|\\partial S_i|$. Reverse the order to get a layout. Its first $n-i$\nvertices are $C\\setminus S_i$, and those with a neighbour among the remaining\nones are exactly $\\partial S_i$. So the cost of the closing order is the vertex\nseparation of the reversed layout plus one, and Lemma~\\ref{lem:F} at the root\nand Theorem~\\ref{thm:vs} finish the proof.\n\\end{proof}\n\nSo a refutation, $\\neg\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)$, may only mean $\\pw(G)>k-1$. The rest\nof the section asks whether the pruning rules preserve that meaning.\n\n\\paragraph{In pathwidth language.} The exact pathwidth literature writes\n$N(T)$ for the border and $d(T)$ for its size\n\\citep[p.~390]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014}; we keep $\\partial T$ and $b(T)$. A\nvertex sequence is \\emph{$w$-feasible} when every prefix has border at most\n$w$, and $\\vs(G)$ is the least $w$ for which some permutation is $w$-feasible.\nTable~\\ref{tab:dictionary} translates each notion of the search into this\nvocabulary. Read it row by row: every ingredient of the customer search is a\nknown ingredient of an exact pathwidth algorithm, and the last two rows are\nthe rules of Section~\\ref{sec:algorithm}.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{The customer search is, ingredient by ingredient, an exact pathwidth\nalgorithm: each notion of the search beside its name in the pathwidth\nliterature. The memo and the definite move are described in\nSection~\\ref{sec:algorithm}.}\n\\label{tab:dictionary}\n\\begin{tabularx}{\\linewidth}{@{}>{\\raggedright}p{5.2cm}>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X@{}}\n\\toprule\nCustomer search & Layout \\\\\n\\midrule\nclosing order from $\\emptyset$ & vertex sequence, read forwards \\\\\nopen stacks $b(T)$ & border size $|\\partial T|$ \\\\\n$\\cost(T,c)$ & $|\\partial(T\\cup\\{c\\})|+1$ \\\\\n$\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)$ & $\\vs(G)\\le k-1$ \\\\\nfree closure $\\cl(T)$ & full set of $T$ \\citep[p.~328]{SuchanVillanger2009} \\\\\nmemo & failure table \\\\\ndefinite move, repaired & commitment to the child \\\\\n\\bottomrule\\end{tabularx}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{The algorithm}\n\\label{sec:algorithm}\n\nAlgorithm~\\ref{alg:search} gives the customer search. It is a depth-first\nsearch over states. At each node it closes the free customers, consults a\ntable of states already refuted, keeps the playable candidates, filters them\nwith the dominance rules and recurses on the survivors.\n\n\\begin{algorithm}[t]\n\\caption{The customer search \\citep{ChuStuckey2009}. It decides $\\Sol_k(S)$\nfor a graph $G=(C,E)$ and a stack bound $k$, and is called as\n$\\textsc{Search}(\\emptyset,\\emptyset)$. $Q$ is the set of old moves the node\ninherits.}\n\\label{alg:search}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}r@{\\quad}l@{\\qquad}l@{}}\n1 & $\\textsc{Search}(S,Q)$: & \\\\\n2 & \\quad $S\\gets\\cl(S)$ & \\textcolor{gray}{free moves (Lemma~\\ref{lem:F})} \\\\\n3 & \\quad \\textbf{if} $S=C$ \\textbf{then return} true & \\\\\n4 & \\quad \\textbf{if} $S$ is in the memo \\textbf{then return} false & \\textcolor{gray}{memo} \\\\\n5 & \\quad $P\\gets$ the $c\\notin S\\cup Q$ with $\\cost(S,c)\\le k$, in index order & \\textcolor{gray}{cost cut} \\\\\n6 & \\quad $L\\gets$ filter of $P$: definite move, then subset rule, then better move & \\textcolor{gray}{Sections~\\ref{sec:definite}--\\ref{sec:other-rules}} \\\\\n7 & \\quad $\\mathit{seen}\\gets Q\\setminus S$ & \\\\\n8 & \\quad \\textbf{for each} $c\\in L$: & \\\\\n9 & \\quad\\quad $Q'\\gets\\{q\\in\\mathit{seen} : \\cost(S\\cup\\{q\\},c)\\le k\\}$ & \\textcolor{gray}{old moves} \\\\\n10 & \\quad\\quad \\textbf{if} $\\textsc{Search}(S\\cup\\{c\\},Q')$ \\textbf{then return} true & \\\\\n11 & \\quad\\quad $\\mathit{seen}\\gets\\mathit{seen}\\cup\\{c\\}$ & \\\\\n12 & \\quad add $S$ to the memo & \\\\\n13 & \\quad \\textbf{return} false & \\\\\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{algorithm}\n\nLine~2 is safe by Lemma~\\ref{lem:F}, and the cost cut of line~5 is exact,\nsince an unplayable move is not a move of $\\Sol_k$. The rest is pruning, and\neach piece has a one-sentence intuition.\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\emph{The memo} (lines~4 and~12): whether a state can be finished does\n  not depend on how it was reached, so a state refuted once is refused when\n  met again.\n\\item \\emph{Old moves} (lines~7--11): a sibling whose subtree failed stays\n  failed further down, as long as closing it first would have kept each later\n  step within the bound.\n\\item \\emph{The definite move}: if closing $q$ now leaves no more stacks open\n  than before, it seems safe to close $q$ at once and try nothing else.\n\\item \\emph{The subset rule}: if $d$ newly opens a subset of what $r$ newly\n  opens, then $d$ is at least as good a move as $r$.\n\\item \\emph{The better move}: if $q$ would be a definite move right after $r$,\n  then closing $q$ before $r$ is at least as good as closing $r$ first.\n\\end{itemize}\nEach of the three filtering rules discards a move $r$ by citing a\n\\emph{covering}, a kept move $c$ with\n$\\Sol_k(S\\cdot r)\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$. The filter runs the definite\nmove, then the subset rule, then the better move, each once. If the definite\nmove fires, it keeps its customer alone.\n\nThis loop is the decision procedure of \\citet[Algorithm~1,\np.~393]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014}, which asks whether a vertex set extends to\na $w$-feasible permutation: replace the set by a committed extension, answer\nfrom a failure table or at the full vertex set, recurse on each feasible\none-vertex extension, and record failures. The customer search is that loop\nwith $w=k-1$. The free closure and the definite move play the part of the\ncommitment, the memo is the failure table, and the cost cut is the feasibility\ntest. \\citet[\\S3.2]{CoudertMazauricNisse2014} has the same three parts in a\nbranch and bound. Neither line cites the other in the papers we hold:\n\\citet{ChuStuckey2009} predates the commitments and both 2014 papers, and none\nof the pathwidth papers cites it. This is the isolation measured in\nTable~\\ref{tab:communities}, at the level of one algorithm.\n\n\\subsection{The definite move is false as published, and its repair is a matching}\n\\label{sec:definite}\n\nThe definite move is the strongest of the rules, since when it fires it\ndiscards every other candidate. Its premise looks like a safe accounting:\nclosing $q$ opens some new stacks and lets some customers close, and if it\nlets at least as many close as it opens, nothing seems lost by closing it\nfirst. \\citet[\\S3.1, p.~6]{ChuStuckey2009} state:\n\\begin{quote}\n\\textbf{Theorem 1.} Suppose S ++ [q] is playable and close(q, S) $\\ge$\nopen(q, S), then if U$'$ = S ++ R is a solution, there exists a solution\nU = S ++ [q] ++ R$'$.\n\\end{quote}\nHere ++ is concatenation of closing sequences, a \\emph{solution} is a complete\nclosing order with every step playable, and $\\close(q,S)$ counts the customers\n$d$ with $o(d,S)\\subseteq o(q,S)$ (p.~4). The rule keeps $q$ alone. We read $d$\nas ranging over customers not yet closed, as our implementation does; read\nliterally, closed customers count too, which weakens the premise, and both\nreadings are refuted below. \\citet[Thm~6.3.6, p.~142]{Chu2011} restates the\ntheorem with the same premise and proof, reworded for $k$ stacks. Write\n$X=S\\cdot q$. The first lemma confirms that the premise says what the\nintuition says.\n\n\\begin{lemma}[what the premise says]\\label{lem:premise}\n$\\close(q,S)=|X\\setminus S|$. Hence the premise holds iff $b(X)\\le b(S)$: the\nchild has no more open stacks than the parent~\\leanref{premise}.\n\\end{lemma}\n\nThe premise compares only the two ends, $S$ and $X$. The rule fails because a\nset between them can be cheaper still. Figure~\\ref{fig:cex} shows the graph on\nwhich it fails.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.85\\linewidth]{definite_counterexample.pdf}\n\\caption{The graph of Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} at the state where the\ndefinite move fails. Customer 2 is closed; 1, 3 and 4 are open. Moving 0 opens\nthe new stacks 0, 7 and 11 and closes 0, 3 and 4, so open = close = 3 and the\nrule keeps 0 alone. But 3 and 4 have the single new stack 0 between them:\nclosing them first gains two stacks for the price of one, which a solution\nstarting with 0 cannot match.}\n\\label{fig:cex}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{counterexample}\\label{cex:definite}\nLet $G$ be the graph of Figure~\\ref{fig:cex}, on customers $0,\\dots,13$ with\nthe 26 edges\n\\begin{gather*}\n0\\text{--}3,\\ 0\\text{--}4,\\ 0\\text{--}7,\\ 0\\text{--}11,\\ 1\\text{--}2,\\ 1\\text{--}5,\\ 1\\text{--}6,\\ 2\\text{--}3,\\ 2\\text{--}4,\\ 5\\text{--}9,\\ 5\\text{--}11,\\ 5\\text{--}12,\\ 6\\text{--}8,\\\\\n6\\text{--}13,\\ 7\\text{--}9,\\ 7\\text{--}10,\\ 7\\text{--}13,\\ 8\\text{--}9,\\ 8\\text{--}10,\\ 8\\text{--}11,\\ 9\\text{--}10,\\ 9\\text{--}11,\\ 10\\text{--}11,\\ 10\\text{--}12,\\ 10\\text{--}13,\\ 12\\text{--}13,\n\\end{gather*}\nwith $S=\\{2\\}$, $k=6$ and $q=0$. Then $O(S)=\\{1,2,3,4\\}$,\n$o(0,S)=\\{0,7,11\\}$ and $\\cost(S,0)=6$, so $0$ is playable. The customers with\n$o(d,S)\\subseteq\\{0,7,11\\}$ are $0$, $3$ and $4$, so $\\close(0,S)=3\\ge\\open(0,S)$,\nand $0$ comes first in index order, so the rule keeps only $0$. A solution\nexists from $S$: the order\n\\[1,3,4,6,12,13,0,5,7,8,9,10,11\\]\ncosts at most 6. None exists from the child $X=\\{0,2,3,4\\}$: only seven sets\nare reachable from $X$ by moves of cost at most 6, and none is $C$~\\leanref{cex}.\n\\end{counterexample}\n\nThe published implication, as a universal statement, is refuted in Lean under\nboth readings of $\\close$, and so is the thesis's Theorem~6.3.6~\\leanref{thm1false}.\n\\citet[pp.~27--28]{Fink2012} restates the rule with a stronger premise,\ncounting only dominated customers of smaller index than $q$. The graph above\nrefutes it under no labelling, but a third twin of 3 and 4 gives a\n15-customer graph that does~\\leanref{fink}. The published proof moves $q$ to\nthe front of a solution and counts, after each prefix, the stacks $q$ opens\nagainst the customers it lets close. But customers that the solution has\nalready closed no longer count as closed by $q$, while the stacks $q$ opens may\nstill be new. Appendix~\\ref{app:whyfails} gives the full analysis, and\nAppendix~\\ref{app:minimality} the search for smaller counterexamples.\n\n\\paragraph{The repair.} The fix is to require the comparison at every set\nbetween the two ends, not only at $S$. Call $q$ \\emph{hereditarily definite} at\n$S$ when $b(X)\\le b(B)$ for every $B$ with $S\\subseteq B\\subseteq X$ and\n$q\\notin B$. By Lemma~\\ref{lem:premise}, the published premise is the case\n$B=S$. In the counterexample the repair fails at $B=\\{2,3,4\\}$, where\n$b(B)=2<3=b(X)$~\\leanref{hered}. The proof that the repair is sound needs one\ninequality.\n\n\\begin{lemma}[open stacks are submodular]\\label{lem:submod}\nFor all $A,B\\subseteq C$, $b(A\\cup B)+b(A\\cap B)\\le b(A)+b(B)$~\\leanref{submod}.\n\\end{lemma}\n\\begin{proof}\n$b(T)=|O(T)|-|T|$, $O(A\\cup B)=O(A)\\cup O(B)$ and $O(A\\cap B)\\subseteq\nO(A)\\cap O(B)$; the customers themselves balance exactly.\n\\end{proof}\n\n\\begin{theorem}[the repaired definite move is sound]\\label{thm:repaired}\nIf $q\\notin S$ is hereditarily definite at $S$, then $P_k(S)\\Rightarrow P_k(X)$.\nAt a state with the invariant, $\\Sol_k(S)\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$~\\leanref{repaired}.\n\\end{theorem}\n\\begin{proof}\nTake an order from $S$ of cost at most $k$ with prefixes $T_i$, in which $q$ is\nthe $j$-th step, and follow it from $X$, skipping customers already in $X$. A\nstep $i<j$ closes $c_i$ at $T_{i-1}\\cup X$. We have\n$S\\subseteq T_i\\cap X\\subseteq X$ and $q\\notin T_i$, so the hypothesis gives\n$b(X)\\le b(T_i\\cap X)$, and Lemma~\\ref{lem:submod} gives\n$b(T_i\\cup X)\\le b(T_i)$: the step costs no more than the original. After step\n$j-1$ the closed set contains $T_j$ with the same opened set, and the rest of\nthe order applies. The search's form follows by Lemma~\\ref{lem:F}.\n\\end{proof}\n\nThis theorem is not new. The hereditary premise is the commitment condition of\nTamaki, and the proof is the proof of the commitment lemma,\n\\citet[Lemma~1, p.~142]{Kitsunai2016}; Section~\\ref{sec:layouts} makes the\ncorrespondence exact.\n\n\\paragraph{The matching test.} The repaired rule seems to require checking\nexponentially many intermediate sets. It does not, and this cheap test is the\nalgorithmic contribution of the section; we did not find it in the papers we\nhold. In words: the rule is safe exactly when all but one of the new stacks\ncan be paired with a different freed customer who needed it. In the\ncounterexample, closing 0 opens three new stacks and frees 3 and 4, both of\nwhom needed only stack 0: one pairing where two are required.\n\n\\begin{theorem}[the repair is a matching condition]\\label{thm:matching}\nLet $Y=X\\setminus(S\\cup\\{q\\})$, and join each $d\\in Y$ to the stacks of\n$o(d,S)\\subseteq o(q,S)$. Then $q$ is hereditarily definite at $S$ iff this\nbipartite graph has a matching with at least $\\open(q,S)-1$ edges~\\leanref{matching}.\n\\end{theorem}\n\\begin{proof}\nThe sets of the repair are $B=S\\cup D$, $D\\subseteq Y$, and\n$b(X)\\le b(S\\cup D)$ iff $|D|-|o(D)|\\le\\delta$ with\n$\\delta=|Y|+1-\\open(q,S)$, where $o(D)$ is the union of the $o(d,S)$. By the\ndeficiency form of Hall's theorem the largest matching has size\n$|Y|-\\max_D(|D|-|o(D)|)$, which is at least $|Y|-\\delta=\\open(q,S)-1$ exactly\nwhen the repair holds.\n\\end{proof}\n\nThe bipartite graph has at most $\\close-1$ customers on one side and $\\open$\nstacks on the other, and the matching can stop at $\\open-1$ edges. Every $q$\nwith $\\open(q,S)\\le1$ passes at once, so the published rule is right\nthere~\\leanref{open1}.\n\n\\subsection{The other rules}\n\\label{sec:other-rules}\n\nEach of the remaining rules is quoted, proved sound in the form used, and\nshown to need the test or tie-break it carries.\n\n\\paragraph{The subset rule.} ``If $o(c_i,S)\\subseteq o(c_j,S)$ and $i<j$ \\dots\\\nmove $j$ can be removed'' \\citep[\\S2, p.~5]{ChuStuckey2009}. As implemented,\n$d$ dominates $r$ when $o(d,S)\\subseteq o(r,S)$ and the inclusion is strict or\n$d<r$. Every dominated playable $r$ is dropped, with a fallback that keeps $P$\nif all would go. The covering is sound for every set $S$: if\n$o(d,S)\\subseteq o(r,S)$ and $r$ is playable, then $d$ is playable and\n$\\Sol_k(S\\cdot r)\\Rightarrow \\Sol_k(S\\cdot d)$, because $d$ is free after $r$\nand closing $d$ then $r$ reaches the same state at no greater cost. With the\nindex tie-break, domination is a strict order, so an undominated move with a\nsolution survives, and the fallback never fires when a solution\nexists~\\leanref{subset}. The tie-break is necessary: without it, two customers\nwith equal new stacks dominate each other and both go, which loses a solution\non a 7-customer graph~\\leanref{tiebreak}.\n\n\\paragraph{The better move.} \\citet[\\S3.2, p.~6]{ChuStuckey2009}: ``Suppose\nS ++ [q] and S ++ [r, q] are playable and close(q, S $\\cup$ \\{r\\}) $\\ge$\nopen(q, S $\\cup$ \\{r\\}) then if U$'$ = S ++ [r] ++ R is a solution there exists a\nsolution U = S ++ [q] ++ R$'$.'' Its premise is the definite move's premise for\n$q$ at the child $\\cl(S\\cup\\{r\\})$, together with playability of $r$ then $q$,\nand its conclusion swaps $q$ and $r$~\\leanref{isbetter}. The swap is sound,\nbecause both orders end at the same state and neither step overflows. The\nappeal to the definite move is not.\n\n\\emph{Counterexample.} On the graph of Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} at the\nroot with $k=6$, the definite move does not fire, customers 0 and 2 both\nsurvive the subset rule, and the premise holds for $r=2$, $q=0$, so the rule\ndrops 2 citing 0. But $\\{2\\}$ has a solution and $\\{0\\}$ has\nnone~\\leanref{bettercex}. At that node another kept customer still has a\nsolution, so this is a false link and not a lost node; no node where the\nbetter move loses the last solution has been found. \\citet[Thm~6.3.8,\np.~143]{Chu2011} states the better move with a different premise,\n$\\close(q,S)\\ge\\open(q,S\\cup\\{r\\})$, and proves it through his Theorem~6.3.6.\nThe same graph refutes it at $S=\\{2\\}$, $r=3$, $q=0$~\\leanref{thm638}.\n\n\\emph{Repair and test.} Cite $q$ for $r$ only if $q$ is hereditarily definite\nat $\\cl(S\\cup\\{r\\})$, which is one matching at the child\n(Theorem~\\ref{thm:matching}); then the swap argument goes\nthrough~\\leanref{repbetter}.\n\n\\emph{Two requirements.} The close count in the premise must not include the\ncustomers that $r$ finishes on its own; counting them makes the rule's own\nconclusion false on a 12-customer graph. And the rules must run in the order\ndefinite, subset, better, each citing only customers still standing: if the\nbetter move runs first, the subset rule can cite a customer the better move\nhas already dropped, and an 8-customer graph loses a node through a cycle of\nlinks that are each true~\\leanref{bugs}. The second is an instance of the\ngeneral observation of \\citet[Prop.~3, Example~6]{Beck2025} that mutually\ndominating transitions must be tie-broken.\n\n\\paragraph{The old move.} \\citet[Thm~3, p.~7]{ChuStuckey2009} let a child\ninherit a sibling $q$ whose subtree failed, provided that inserting $q$ before\neach later step keeps that step playable (line~9 of\nAlgorithm~\\ref{alg:search}). The rule is sound as implemented: if\n$\\cost(S\\cup\\{q\\},c)\\le k$, then\n$\\Sol_k(\\cl(\\{q\\}\\cup S\\cdot c))\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$, so a refuted\nsibling stays refuted along any path whose steps pass the test~\\leanref{oldmove}.\nThe test is necessary; the smallest graph that shows it is the path on five\nvertices~\\leanref{reinsert}.\n\n\\paragraph{The memo.} Whether a state with $b(S)\\le k$ has a solution depends\non the state alone, not on the path that reached it~\\leanref{memo}. One\nconcern is that the memo and the old move might not combine: a refutation\nobtained with inherited old moves seems to depend on the ancestors, and one\nimplementation of the search declines to run both for that reason. A node\ntaken on its own, with an old move that is not in fact refuted, can indeed\nrecord a solvable state~\\leanref{fakeold}. Theorem~\\ref{thm:runs}\n(Appendix~\\ref{app:machinery}) shows that this never happens in a real run,\nbecause every old move is a refutation completed earlier in the same run.\n\n\\subsection{The repaired search is sound}\n\\label{sec:theorem}\n\n\\paragraph{What a rule must satisfy.} At a state $S$ with old moves $Q$, the\ncost cut keeps the playable candidates $P$ and the filter keeps a sublist\n$L\\subseteq P$. The filter is \\emph{node-sound} at $(S,Q)$ if, whenever every\n$q\\in Q$ has $\\neg\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$, $\\Sol_k(S)$ implies $\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$ for\nsome $c\\in L$~\\leanref{nodesound}. In terms of coverings, a node is sound when\ntwo things hold. First, every link a rule cites is a true covering. Second,\nevery chain of links from a discarded candidate ends in $L$ or in $Q$. The\nsecond condition can fail through a cycle even when every link is true, which\nis why the order of the rules matters. Proposition~\\ref{prop:filter} shows that\nthe repaired filter is node-sound, and Theorem~\\ref{thm:runs} that node\nsoundness, the memo and the old move together give sound runs; both are in\nAppendix~\\ref{app:machinery}. Together they give the main theorem.\n\n\\begin{theorem}[the revised search is sound]\\label{thm:main}\nRun the customer search with free moves, the memo, old moves and the filter\ndefinite $\\to$ subset $\\to$ better, the definite move firing only on a\nhereditarily definite customer and the better move citing only under the\nrepaired premise. If it refutes $k$ from the root, then no closing order costs\nat most $k$, $\\pw(G)>k-1$, and on the MOSP graph of an instance $M$ with at\nleast one 1, $Z(M)>k$~\\leanref{main}.\n\\end{theorem}\n\nIn words: each rule throws away only moves that a kept move is proved to be at\nleast as good as, and the memo and the old moves remember only failures that\nreally happened earlier in the same search. So when the search says ``no'',\nno closing order fits in $k$ stacks.\n\n\\paragraph{What is proved and what is tested.} The theorem is about the search\nas specified; that our C and Python implementations run it is checked, not\nproved. Three checks link them:\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item a transcription of the Lean filter agreed with the implemented filter on\n  16,244,090 node checks over graphs on 1 to 17 vertices;\n\\item the matching agreed with the hereditary premise on 36,939,226 candidates;\n\\item a search written from the theorems agreed with both implementations and\n  an exact oracle on 17,431,232 runs over 52,592 graphs on 1 to 17 vertices.\n\\end{itemize}\nSection~\\ref{sec:results} adds a certificate checker for individual\nrefutations.\n\n\\paragraph{The published rules.} Under the published rules the filter is not\nnode-sound~\\leanref{notsound}. In a check of 570,206 runs with memo and old\nmove, it lost the last solution at a node in 58 runs, all on graphs built\naround Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} and all at $k$ equal to the optimum.\nEvery one found a solution through another branch and answered correctly. No\nwhole instance on which the published rules answer \\emph{unsat} wrongly is\nknown.\n\n\\subsection{The rules in pathwidth language}\n\\label{sec:layouts}\n\nThe definite move has an exact counterpart in the pathwidth literature, and\nthis subsection makes the correspondence precise.\n\\citet[p.~142]{Kitsunai2016}, stating Tamaki's notion, call an extension $\\tau$\nof a vertex sequence $\\sigma$ \\emph{committable} when\n$|\\partial X'|\\ge|\\partial V(\\tau)|$ for every $X'$ with\n$V(\\sigma)\\subseteq X'\\subseteq V(\\tau)$. Their Lemma~1 says that if $\\sigma$\nextends to a feasible permutation, so does $\\tau$.\n\n\\begin{lemma}\\label{lem:endpoint}\nFor $q\\notin S$ and $X=\\cl(S\\cup\\{q\\})$: the published premise holds iff\n$b(X)\\le b(S)$, the commitment condition at the single set $X'=S$; and the\nrepaired premise holds iff $X$ is a commitment from $S$, the condition at every\n$X'$ between them~\\leanref{endpoint}.\n\\end{lemma}\n\nSo Theorem~\\ref{thm:repaired} is the commitment lemma at the child, and the\npublished definite move is a commitment whose interior condition was dropped.\nIn Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} the endpoint test passes with\n$b(X)=3=b(S)$, while the interior set $\\{2,3,4\\}$ has border\n2~\\leanref{cexnotcommit}. The published premise is not wasted: by\n\\citet[Lemma~10, Cor.~2, pp.~148--149]{Kitsunai2016} it guarantees a\ncommitment to the least-border set between $S$ and $X$, which need not contain\n$q$~\\leanref{existscommit}. In the counterexample that set is\n$\\{2,3,4\\}$~\\leanref{commit234}. Committing there when the matching fails is a\nsecond repair that gives up no pruning; we have not built it.\n\nA commitment of depth one, $S$ to $S\\cup\\{q\\}$, holds iff\n$\\open(q,S)\\le1$~\\leanref{depth1}. The greedy step of\n\\citet[Lemma~3, p.~49]{CoudertMazauricNisse2014} is exactly that\ncondition~\\leanref{greedy}, and the push rule of\n\\citet[Rule~1, p.~330]{SuchanVillanger2009} is a case of it. So every published\npathwidth rule of this kind lies in the region $\\open\\le1$, where the published\ndefinite move is right; \\citet[p.~388, Table~2]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014}\nreport depth-one commitments as ``extremely effective''. Chu and Stuckey's\ndefinite move reaches into $\\open\\ge2$, and that is where it fails. For the\ncost of the test, \\citet[Cor.~2]{Kitsunai2016} find the least border between\nthe ends of a given extension by a minimum $s$--$t$ separator;\nTheorem~\\ref{thm:matching} is that computation for the single target $X$, where\nit becomes a bipartite matching.\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:known} sums up, and its rows are grouped by what they say. The\npublished definite and better moves are false. The free move and the repaired\ndefinite move are known pathwidth results. The subset rule, the repaired\nbetter move and the old move have no counterpart we found, and are proved\nhere, as is the matching test. The comparison is limited to the pathwidth\npapers we hold\n\\citep{SuchanVillanger2009,Bodlaender2012,CoudertMazauricNisse2014,CoudertMazauricNisse2016,KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014,Kitsunai2016};\n``none found'' is not a claim of priority.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{Two published rules are false, two repaired or reused rules are known\npathwidth results, and the rest, with the matching test, are proved here: each\nrule of the customer search against the exact-pathwidth literature.}\n\\label{tab:known}\n\\begin{tabularx}{\\linewidth}{@{}>{\\raggedright}p{3.2cm}>{\\raggedright}X>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}p{4.1cm}@{}}\n\\toprule\nRule & Published counterpart & Status \\\\\n\\midrule\ndefinite move, published & -- & false (Cex.~\\ref{cex:definite}) \\\\\nbetter move, published & none found & false \\\\\n\\midrule\nfree move & full sets \\citep{SuchanVillanger2009}; \\citet[Prop.~3]{Kitsunai2016} & known \\\\\ndefinite move, $\\open\\le1$ & depth-one commitments; greedy step; push rule & known \\\\\ndefinite move, repaired & commitment lemma \\citep[Lemma~1]{Kitsunai2016} & known; matching test (Thm~\\ref{thm:matching}) not found \\\\\nmemo & subset DP \\citep{Bodlaender2012}; prefix and failure tables & known; with the old move, proved here (Thm~\\ref{thm:runs}) \\\\\n\\midrule\nsubset rule & none found & proved here \\\\\nbetter move, repaired & none found & proved here \\\\\nold move & none found & proved here \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabularx}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Prior reports.} We found no earlier report of the error, in 56 of\nthe 141 works that cite the customer search, read in full\n(Appendix~\\ref{app:prior}). The pathwidth literature has the correct form of\nthe rule, as a commitment, and none of the pathwidth papers we hold mentions\nMOSP.\n"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014B34mAmT1NyGW4qTLtLXC9","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/al/dev/MOSP/paper2/latex/sec4_search.tex has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a1c55f7b-98f8-4ea9-815f-35ebfa760af6","uuid":"1fb84eab-1992-448d-97d1-90df10478cd0","timestamp":"2026-10-06T16:47:10.986Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper2/latex/sec4_search.tex","content":"% Section 4. Sources: paper2/revised_algorithm.md (4.1-4.6), search_soundness.md,\n% prior_art_counterexample.md (prior art). Lean: lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/,\n% names in Appendix A (sec9a_appendix_lean.tex). The old 4.6-4.7 are Section 5.\n% Restructured 2026-10-06 (loop0009 item 05, review changes 4 and 5): stakes\n% first, the pathwidth reading and the dictionary in 4.1, Algorithm 4.1 in 4.2,\n% each rule as intuition -> counterexample -> repair -> test.\n% Page numbers for Chu & Stuckey (2009) are those of the preprint\n% literature/chu_stuckey_2009.pdf; for Chu (2011) the printed ones.\n\\section{An exact open-stacks search, read as a pathwidth algorithm}\n\\label{sec:search}\n\nIf MOSP is pathwidth (Theorem~\\ref{thm:mosp}), then the best exact MOSP\nalgorithm is a pathwidth algorithm, and its rules can be checked against what\nthe pathwidth literature knows. This section does that for the \\emph{customer\nsearch} of \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}, the search that Section~\\ref{sec:names}\ntraced from the open-stacks challenge. It searches over the orders in which the\ncustomers' stacks close, not over pattern sequences, and it prunes with\ndominance rules. Three of the rules are stated as theorems (their Theorems~1,\n2 and~3) and a fourth, the subset rule, in their \\S2. \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}\nplace MOSP among ``graph path-width and gate matrix layout'', citing\n\\citet{LY2002}, but they do not use pathwidth in the search. Any graph is the\nMOSP graph of the instance with one customer per vertex and one pattern per\nedge, so the search decides pathwidth for every graph.\n\nThe stakes are these. Two of the published dominance theorems, the\n\\emph{definite move} and the \\emph{better move}, are false, and so are their\nrestatements in Chu's thesis \\citep{Chu2011} and, with a stronger premise, in\n\\citet{Fink2012}. Yet no wrong answer is known: no whole instance on which the\npublished rules answer ``no'' wrongly has been found, and when every benchmark\noptimum that rested on the search was proved again with repaired rules, no\nvalue changed (Section~\\ref{sec:results}). The correct form of the definite\nmove was already known for pathwidth, as Tamaki's commitment lemma, and we\nshow that it can be tested by one small bipartite matching per candidate. The\nmain result, Theorem~\\ref{thm:main}, says informally: \\emph{with both rules\nrepaired, the search is sound; whenever it answers that $k$ stacks do not\nsuffice, they do not, and the graph has pathwidth at least $k$.} The repair\ncosts at most 2\\% more search nodes on each benchmark set\n(Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n\nSection~\\ref{sec:search-def} defines the search and shows that it decides\npathwidth, in both vocabularies. Section~\\ref{sec:algorithm} gives the\nalgorithm. Sections~\\ref{sec:definite} and~\\ref{sec:other-rules} take the\nrules one at a time, Section~\\ref{sec:theorem} proves the repaired search\nsound, and Section~\\ref{sec:layouts} places each rule in the pathwidth\nliterature.\n\n\\subsection{The search decides pathwidth}\n\\label{sec:search-def}\n\n\\citet[p.~4]{ChuStuckey2009} observe that the patterns matter only through the\nMOSP graph. So fix a graph $G=(C,E)$ whose vertices are the customers, and an\ninteger $k\\ge0$. In this section $k$ bounds the number of open stacks, which is\none more than a width: $k$ stacks suffice exactly when the pathwidth is at most\n$k-1$ (Theorem~\\ref{thm:decides}). For a customer $c$ write $N[c]$ for its\nclosed neighbourhood, the stacks that must be open when $c$'s stack closes. For\na set $T\\subseteq C$ of vertices, its \\emph{border} $\\partial T$ is the set of\nvertices outside $T$ with a neighbour in $T$. For a set $T$ of closed\ncustomers write\n\\begin{align*}\nO(T) &= \\textstyle\\bigcup_{c\\in T}N[c] && \\text{the stacks opened once $T$ has closed},\\\\\n\\fin(X) &= \\{c : N[c]\\subseteq X\\} && \\text{the customers free to close when $X$ is open},\\\\\n\\cl(T) &= \\fin(O(T)) && \\text{the \\emph{free closure} of $T$},\\\\\nb(T) &= |O(T)\\setminus T| = |\\partial T| && \\text{the stacks open after $T$ has closed},\\\\\no(c,T) &= N[c]\\setminus O(T),\\quad \\open(c,T)=|o(c,T)| && \\text{the stacks closing $c$ would newly open.}\n\\end{align*}\nThe open stacks after $T$ has closed are exactly the border of $T$, and this\none identity carries the whole translation to pathwidth.\n\nA \\emph{move} at $T$ closes one customer $c\\notin T$, at cost\n$\\cost(T,c)=|O(T\\cup\\{c\\})\\setminus T| = b(T\\cup\\{c\\})+1$, the stacks open at\nthat moment counting $c$. It is \\emph{playable} when $\\cost(T,c)\\le k$. Write\n$P_k(T)$ when some order of $C\\setminus T$ has every step playable. On entering\na node the search closes every free customer, so it stands only at\n\\emph{states}, sets with $S=\\cl(S)$. The \\emph{child} of $S$ by $c$ is\n$S\\cdot c=\\cl(S\\cup\\{c\\})$. The search's predicate $\\Sol_k(S)$ holds when\n$S=C$, or when some playable $c$ has $\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$~\\leanref{defs}. Every\nnode the search visits satisfies the invariant $b(S)\\le k$.\n\n\\paragraph{An example.} Take the graph of Figure~\\ref{fig:example}(d), with\n$k=3$. At $T=\\{a\\}$ we have $O(T)=\\{a,b\\}$, so one stack, $b$, is open:\n$b(T)=1$ and $\\partial T=\\{b\\}$. No other customer is free, since $N[b]$\ncontains $c$ and $d$, so $T$ is a state. Closing $b$ newly opens\n$o(b,T)=\\{c,d\\}$ and costs $\\cost(T,b)=3$, the stacks $b$, $c$ and $d$; the\nmove is playable. At $\\{a,b,c\\}$ every neighbour of $d$ is open, so $d$ is\nfree, and the child $\\{a,b\\}\\cdot c$ is $\\{a,b,c,d\\}$. The closing order\n$a,b,c,e$ (with $d$ and $f$ closed free) costs $2,3,3,2$, the profile of\norder~A in Figure~\\ref{fig:example}(a).\n\n\\begin{lemma}[free moves never hurt]\\label{lem:F}\nIf $b(T)\\le k$, then $P_k(T)\\iff\\Sol_k(\\cl(T))$~\\leanref{F}.\n\\end{lemma}\nClosing a customer whose neighbourhood is already open opens nothing, never\nmakes a later step dearer, and leaves one fewer stack open. The hypothesis is\nneeded: closing the free customers one by one costs $b(T)$ at each step.\n\n\\begin{theorem}[the search decides pathwidth]\\label{thm:decides}\nFor nonempty $C$, $\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)\\iff\\pw(G)+1\\le k$. On the MOSP graph of an\ninstance $M$ with at least one 1, $\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)\\iff Z(M)\\le k$~\\leanref{decides}.\n\\end{theorem}\n\\begin{proof}\nFor a closing order $c_1,\\dots,c_n$ with prefixes $S_i$, the $i$-th step costs\n$1+|\\partial S_i|$. Reverse the order to get a layout. Its first $n-i$\nvertices are $C\\setminus S_i$, and those with a neighbour among the remaining\nones are exactly $\\partial S_i$. So the cost of the closing order is the vertex\nseparation of the reversed layout plus one, and Lemma~\\ref{lem:F} at the root\nand Theorem~\\ref{thm:vs} finish the proof.\n\\end{proof}\n\nSo a refutation, $\\neg\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)$, may only mean $\\pw(G)>k-1$. The rest\nof the section asks whether the pruning rules preserve that meaning.\n\n\\paragraph{In pathwidth language.} The exact pathwidth literature writes\n$N(T)$ for the border and $d(T)$ for its size\n\\citep[p.~390]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014}; we keep $\\partial T$ and $b(T)$. A\nvertex sequence is \\emph{$w$-feasible} when every prefix has border at most\n$w$, and $\\vs(G)$ is the least $w$ for which some permutation is $w$-feasible.\nTable~\\ref{tab:dictionary} translates each notion of the search into this\nvocabulary. Read it row by row: every ingredient of the customer search is a\nknown ingredient of an exact pathwidth algorithm, and the last two rows are\nthe rules of Section~\\ref{sec:algorithm}.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{The customer search is, ingredient by ingredient, an exact pathwidth\nalgorithm: each notion of the search beside its name in the pathwidth\nliterature. The memo and the definite move are described in\nSection~\\ref{sec:algorithm}.}\n\\label{tab:dictionary}\n\\begin{tabularx}{\\linewidth}{@{}>{\\raggedright}p{5.2cm}>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X@{}}\n\\toprule\nCustomer search & Layout \\\\\n\\midrule\nclosing order from $\\emptyset$ & vertex sequence, read forwards \\\\\nopen stacks $b(T)$ & border size $|\\partial T|$ \\\\\n$\\cost(T,c)$ & $|\\partial(T\\cup\\{c\\})|+1$ \\\\\n$\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)$ & $\\vs(G)\\le k-1$ \\\\\nfree closure $\\cl(T)$ & full set of $T$ \\citep[p.~328]{SuchanVillanger2009} \\\\\nmemo & failure table \\\\\ndefinite move, repaired & commitment to the child \\\\\n\\bottomrule\\end{tabularx}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{The algorithm}\n\\label{sec:algorithm}\n\nAlgorithm~\\ref{alg:search} gives the customer search. It is a depth-first\nsearch over states. At each node it closes the free customers, consults a\ntable of states already refuted, keeps the playable candidates, filters them\nwith the dominance rules and recurses on the survivors.\n\n\\begin{algorithm}[t]\n\\caption{The customer search \\citep{ChuStuckey2009}. It decides $\\Sol_k(S)$\nfor a graph $G=(C,E)$ and a stack bound $k$, and is called as\n$\\textsc{Search}(\\emptyset,\\emptyset)$. $Q$ is the set of old moves the node\ninherits.}\n\\label{alg:search}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}r@{\\quad}l@{\\qquad}l@{}}\n1 & $\\textsc{Search}(S,Q)$: & \\\\\n2 & \\quad $S\\gets\\cl(S)$ & \\textcolor{gray}{free moves (Lemma~\\ref{lem:F})} \\\\\n3 & \\quad \\textbf{if} $S=C$ \\textbf{then return} true & \\\\\n4 & \\quad \\textbf{if} $S$ is in the memo \\textbf{then return} false & \\textcolor{gray}{memo} \\\\\n5 & \\quad $P\\gets$ the $c\\notin S\\cup Q$ with $\\cost(S,c)\\le k$, in index order & \\textcolor{gray}{cost cut} \\\\\n6 & \\quad $L\\gets$ filter of $P$: definite move, then subset rule, then better move & \\textcolor{gray}{Sections~\\ref{sec:definite}--\\ref{sec:other-rules}} \\\\\n7 & \\quad $\\mathit{seen}\\gets Q\\setminus S$ & \\\\\n8 & \\quad \\textbf{for each} $c\\in L$: & \\\\\n9 & \\quad\\quad $Q'\\gets\\{q\\in\\mathit{seen} : \\cost(S\\cup\\{q\\},c)\\le k\\}$ & \\textcolor{gray}{old moves} \\\\\n10 & \\quad\\quad \\textbf{if} $\\textsc{Search}(S\\cup\\{c\\},Q')$ \\textbf{then return} true & \\\\\n11 & \\quad\\quad $\\mathit{seen}\\gets\\mathit{seen}\\cup\\{c\\}$ & \\\\\n12 & \\quad add $S$ to the memo & \\\\\n13 & \\quad \\textbf{return} false & \\\\\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{algorithm}\n\nLine~2 is safe by Lemma~\\ref{lem:F}, and the cost cut of line~5 is exact,\nsince an unplayable move is not a move of $\\Sol_k$. The rest is pruning, and\neach piece has a one-sentence intuition.\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\emph{The memo} (lines~4 and~12): whether a state can be finished does\n  not depend on how it was reached, so a state refuted once is refused when\n  met again.\n\\item \\emph{Old moves} (lines~7--11): a sibling whose subtree failed stays\n  failed further down, as long as closing it first would have kept each later\n  step within the bound.\n\\item \\emph{The definite move}: if closing $q$ now leaves no more stacks open\n  than before, it seems safe to close $q$ at once and try nothing else.\n\\item \\emph{The subset rule}: if $d$ newly opens a subset of what $r$ newly\n  opens, then $d$ is at least as good a move as $r$.\n\\item \\emph{The better move}: if $q$ would be a definite move right after $r$,\n  then closing $q$ before $r$ is at least as good as closing $r$ first.\n\\end{itemize}\nEach of the three filtering rules discards a move $r$ by citing a\n\\emph{covering}, a kept move $c$ with\n$\\Sol_k(S\\cdot r)\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$. The filter runs the definite\nmove, then the subset rule, then the better move, each once. If the definite\nmove fires, it keeps its customer alone.\n\nThis loop is the decision procedure of \\citet[Algorithm~1,\np.~393]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014}, which asks whether a vertex set extends to\na $w$-feasible permutation: replace the set by a committed extension, answer\nfrom a failure table or at the full vertex set, recurse on each feasible\none-vertex extension, and record failures. The customer search is that loop\nwith $w=k-1$. The free closure and the definite move play the part of the\ncommitment, the memo is the failure table, and the cost cut is the feasibility\ntest. \\citet[\\S3.2]{CoudertMazauricNisse2014} has the same three parts in a\nbranch and bound. Neither line cites the other in the papers we hold:\n\\citet{ChuStuckey2009} predates the commitments and both 2014 papers, and none\nof the pathwidth papers cites it. This is the isolation measured in\nTable~\\ref{tab:communities}, at the level of one algorithm.\n\n\\subsection{The definite move is false as published, and its repair is a matching}\n\\label{sec:definite}\n\nThe definite move is the strongest of the rules, since when it fires it\ndiscards every other candidate. Its premise looks like a safe accounting:\nclosing $q$ opens some new stacks and lets some customers close, and if it\nlets at least as many close as it opens, nothing seems lost by closing it\nfirst. \\citet[\\S3.1, p.~6]{ChuStuckey2009} state:\n\\begin{quote}\n\\textbf{Theorem 1.} Suppose S ++ [q] is playable and close(q, S) $\\ge$\nopen(q, S), then if U$'$ = S ++ R is a solution, there exists a solution\nU = S ++ [q] ++ R$'$.\n\\end{quote}\nHere ++ is concatenation of closing sequences, a \\emph{solution} is a complete\nclosing order with every step playable, and $\\close(q,S)$ counts the customers\n$d$ with $o(d,S)\\subseteq o(q,S)$ (p.~4). The rule keeps $q$ alone. We read $d$\nas ranging over customers not yet closed, as our implementation does; read\nliterally, closed customers count too, which weakens the premise, and both\nreadings are refuted below. \\citet[Thm~6.3.6, p.~142]{Chu2011} restates the\ntheorem with the same premise and proof, reworded for $k$ stacks. Write\n$X=S\\cdot q$. The first lemma confirms that the premise says what the\nintuition says.\n\n\\begin{lemma}[what the premise says]\\label{lem:premise}\n$\\close(q,S)=|X\\setminus S|$. Hence the premise holds iff $b(X)\\le b(S)$: the\nchild has no more open stacks than the parent~\\leanref{premise}.\n\\end{lemma}\n\nThe premise compares only the two ends, $S$ and $X$. The rule fails because a\nset between them can be cheaper still. Figure~\\ref{fig:cex} shows the graph on\nwhich it fails.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.85\\linewidth]{definite_counterexample.pdf}\n\\caption{The graph of Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} at the state where the\ndefinite move fails. Customer 2 is closed; 1, 3 and 4 are open. Moving 0 opens\nthe new stacks 0, 7 and 11 and closes 0, 3 and 4, so open = close = 3 and the\nrule keeps 0 alone. But 3 and 4 have the single new stack 0 between them:\nclosing them first gains two stacks for the price of one, which a solution\nstarting with 0 cannot match.}\n\\label{fig:cex}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{counterexample}\\label{cex:definite}\nLet $G$ be the graph of Figure~\\ref{fig:cex}, on customers $0,\\dots,13$ with\nthe 26 edges\n\\begin{gather*}\n0\\text{--}3,\\ 0\\text{--}4,\\ 0\\text{--}7,\\ 0\\text{--}11,\\ 1\\text{--}2,\\ 1\\text{--}5,\\ 1\\text{--}6,\\ 2\\text{--}3,\\ 2\\text{--}4,\\ 5\\text{--}9,\\ 5\\text{--}11,\\ 5\\text{--}12,\\ 6\\text{--}8,\\\\\n6\\text{--}13,\\ 7\\text{--}9,\\ 7\\text{--}10,\\ 7\\text{--}13,\\ 8\\text{--}9,\\ 8\\text{--}10,\\ 8\\text{--}11,\\ 9\\text{--}10,\\ 9\\text{--}11,\\ 10\\text{--}11,\\ 10\\text{--}12,\\ 10\\text{--}13,\\ 12\\text{--}13,\n\\end{gather*}\nwith $S=\\{2\\}$, $k=6$ and $q=0$. Then $O(S)=\\{1,2,3,4\\}$,\n$o(0,S)=\\{0,7,11\\}$ and $\\cost(S,0)=6$, so $0$ is playable. The customers with\n$o(d,S)\\subseteq\\{0,7,11\\}$ are $0$, $3$ and $4$, so $\\close(0,S)=3\\ge\\open(0,S)$,\nand $0$ comes first in index order, so the rule keeps only $0$. A solution\nexists from $S$: the order\n\\[1,3,4,6,12,13,0,5,7,8,9,10,11\\]\ncosts at most 6. None exists from the child $X=\\{0,2,3,4\\}$: only seven sets\nare reachable from $X$ by moves of cost at most 6, and none is $C$~\\leanref{cex}.\n\\end{counterexample}\n\nThe published implication, as a universal statement, is refuted in Lean under\nboth readings of $\\close$, and so is the thesis's Theorem~6.3.6~\\leanref{thm1false}.\n\\citet[pp.~27--28]{Fink2012} restates the rule with a stronger premise,\ncounting only dominated customers of smaller index than $q$. The graph above\nrefutes it under no labelling, but a third twin of 3 and 4 gives a\n15-customer graph that does~\\leanref{fink}. The published proof moves $q$ to\nthe front of a solution and counts, after each prefix, the stacks $q$ opens\nagainst the customers it lets close. But customers that the solution has\nalready closed no longer count as closed by $q$, while the stacks $q$ opens may\nstill be new. Appendix~\\ref{app:whyfails} gives the full analysis, and\nAppendix~\\ref{app:minimality} the search for smaller counterexamples.\n\n\\paragraph{The repair.} The fix is to require the comparison at every set\nbetween the two ends, not only at $S$. Call $q$ \\emph{hereditarily definite} at\n$S$ when $b(X)\\le b(B)$ for every $B$ with $S\\subseteq B\\subseteq X$ and\n$q\\notin B$. By Lemma~\\ref{lem:premise}, the published premise is the case\n$B=S$. In the counterexample the repair fails at $B=\\{2,3,4\\}$, where\n$b(B)=2<3=b(X)$~\\leanref{hered}. The proof that the repair is sound needs one\ninequality.\n\n\\begin{lemma}[open stacks are submodular]\\label{lem:submod}\nFor all $A,B\\subseteq C$, $b(A\\cup B)+b(A\\cap B)\\le b(A)+b(B)$~\\leanref{submod}.\n\\end{lemma}\n\\begin{proof}\n$b(T)=|O(T)|-|T|$, $O(A\\cup B)=O(A)\\cup O(B)$ and $O(A\\cap B)\\subseteq\nO(A)\\cap O(B)$; the customers themselves balance exactly.\n\\end{proof}\n\n\\begin{theorem}[the repaired definite move is sound]\\label{thm:repaired}\nIf $q\\notin S$ is hereditarily definite at $S$, then $P_k(S)\\Rightarrow P_k(X)$.\nAt a state with the invariant, $\\Sol_k(S)\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$~\\leanref{repaired}.\n\\end{theorem}\n\\begin{proof}\nTake an order from $S$ of cost at most $k$ with prefixes $T_i$, in which $q$ is\nthe $j$-th step, and follow it from $X$, skipping customers already in $X$. A\nstep $i<j$ closes $c_i$ at $T_{i-1}\\cup X$. We have\n$S\\subseteq T_i\\cap X\\subseteq X$ and $q\\notin T_i$, so the hypothesis gives\n$b(X)\\le b(T_i\\cap X)$, and Lemma~\\ref{lem:submod} gives\n$b(T_i\\cup X)\\le b(T_i)$: the step costs no more than the original. After step\n$j-1$ the closed set contains $T_j$ with the same opened set, and the rest of\nthe order applies. The search's form follows by Lemma~\\ref{lem:F}.\n\\end{proof}\n\nThis theorem is not new. The hereditary premise is the commitment condition of\nTamaki, and the proof is the proof of the commitment lemma,\n\\citet[Lemma~1, p.~142]{Kitsunai2016}; Section~\\ref{sec:layouts} makes the\ncorrespondence exact.\n\n\\paragraph{The matching test.} The repaired rule seems to require checking\nexponentially many intermediate sets. It does not, and this cheap test is the\nalgorithmic contribution of the section; we did not find it in the papers we\nhold. In words: the rule is safe exactly when all but one of the new stacks\ncan be paired with a different freed customer who needed it. In the\ncounterexample, closing 0 opens three new stacks and frees 3 and 4, both of\nwhom needed only stack 0: one pairing where two are required.\n\n\\begin{theorem}[the repair is a matching condition]\\label{thm:matching}\nLet $Y=X\\setminus(S\\cup\\{q\\})$, and join each $d\\in Y$ to the stacks of\n$o(d,S)\\subseteq o(q,S)$. Then $q$ is hereditarily definite at $S$ iff this\nbipartite graph has a matching with at least $\\open(q,S)-1$ edges~\\leanref{matching}.\n\\end{theorem}\n\\begin{proof}\nThe sets of the repair are $B=S\\cup D$, $D\\subseteq Y$, and\n$b(X)\\le b(S\\cup D)$ iff $|D|-|o(D)|\\le\\delta$ with\n$\\delta=|Y|+1-\\open(q,S)$, where $o(D)$ is the union of the $o(d,S)$. By the\ndeficiency form of Hall's theorem the largest matching has size\n$|Y|-\\max_D(|D|-|o(D)|)$, which is at least $|Y|-\\delta=\\open(q,S)-1$ exactly\nwhen the repair holds.\n\\end{proof}\n\nThe bipartite graph has at most $\\close-1$ customers on one side and $\\open$\nstacks on the other, and the matching can stop at $\\open-1$ edges. Every $q$\nwith $\\open(q,S)\\le1$ passes at once, so the published rule is right\nthere~\\leanref{open1}.\n\n\\subsection{The other rules}\n\\label{sec:other-rules}\n\nEach of the remaining rules is quoted, proved sound in the form used, and\nshown to need the test or tie-break it carries.\n\n\\paragraph{The subset rule.} ``If $o(c_i,S)\\subseteq o(c_j,S)$ and $i<j$ \\dots\\\nmove $j$ can be removed'' \\citep[\\S2, p.~5]{ChuStuckey2009}. As implemented,\n$d$ dominates $r$ when $o(d,S)\\subseteq o(r,S)$ and the inclusion is strict or\n$d<r$. Every dominated playable $r$ is dropped, with a fallback that keeps $P$\nif all would go. The covering is sound for every set $S$: if\n$o(d,S)\\subseteq o(r,S)$ and $r$ is playable, then $d$ is playable and\n$\\Sol_k(S\\cdot r)\\Rightarrow \\Sol_k(S\\cdot d)$, because $d$ is free after $r$\nand closing $d$ then $r$ reaches the same state at no greater cost. With the\nindex tie-break, domination is a strict order, so an undominated move with a\nsolution survives, and the fallback never fires when a solution\nexists~\\leanref{subset}. The tie-break is necessary: without it, two customers\nwith equal new stacks dominate each other and both go, which loses a solution\non a 7-customer graph~\\leanref{tiebreak}.\n\n\\paragraph{The better move.} \\citet[\\S3.2, p.~6]{ChuStuckey2009}: ``Suppose\nS ++ [q] and S ++ [r, q] are playable and close(q, S $\\cup$ \\{r\\}) $\\ge$\nopen(q, S $\\cup$ \\{r\\}) then if U$'$ = S ++ [r] ++ R is a solution there exists a\nsolution U = S ++ [q] ++ R$'$.'' Its premise is the definite move's premise for\n$q$ at the child $\\cl(S\\cup\\{r\\})$, together with playability of $r$ then $q$,\nand its conclusion swaps $q$ and $r$~\\leanref{isbetter}. The swap is sound,\nbecause both orders end at the same state and neither step overflows. The\nappeal to the definite move is not.\n\n\\emph{Counterexample.} On the graph of Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} at the\nroot with $k=6$, the definite move does not fire, customers 0 and 2 both\nsurvive the subset rule, and the premise holds for $r=2$, $q=0$, so the rule\ndrops 2 citing 0. But $\\{2\\}$ has a solution and $\\{0\\}$ has\nnone~\\leanref{bettercex}. At that node another kept customer still has a\nsolution, so this is a false link and not a lost node; no node where the\nbetter move loses the last solution has been found. \\citet[Thm~6.3.8,\np.~143]{Chu2011} states the better move with a different premise,\n$\\close(q,S)\\ge\\open(q,S\\cup\\{r\\})$, and proves it through his Theorem~6.3.6.\nThe same graph refutes it at $S=\\{2\\}$, $r=3$, $q=0$~\\leanref{thm638}.\n\n\\emph{Repair and test.} Cite $q$ for $r$ only if $q$ is hereditarily definite\nat $\\cl(S\\cup\\{r\\})$, which is one matching at the child\n(Theorem~\\ref{thm:matching}); then the swap argument goes\nthrough~\\leanref{repbetter}.\n\n\\emph{Two requirements.} The close count in the premise must not include the\ncustomers that $r$ finishes on its own; counting them makes the rule's own\nconclusion false on a 12-customer graph. And the rules must run in the order\ndefinite, subset, better, each citing only customers still standing: if the\nbetter move runs first, the subset rule can cite a customer the better move\nhas already dropped, and an 8-customer graph loses a node through a cycle of\nlinks that are each true~\\leanref{bugs}. The second is an instance of the\ngeneral observation of \\citet[Prop.~3, Example~6]{Beck2025} that mutually\ndominating transitions must be tie-broken.\n\n\\paragraph{The old move.} \\citet[Thm~3, p.~7]{ChuStuckey2009} let a child\ninherit a sibling $q$ whose subtree failed, provided that inserting $q$ before\neach later step keeps that step playable (line~9 of\nAlgorithm~\\ref{alg:search}). The rule is sound as implemented: if\n$\\cost(S\\cup\\{q\\},c)\\le k$, then\n$\\Sol_k(\\cl(\\{q\\}\\cup S\\cdot c))\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$, so a refuted\nsibling stays refuted along any path whose steps pass the test~\\leanref{oldmove}.\nThe test is necessary; the smallest graph that shows it is the path on five\nvertices~\\leanref{reinsert}.\n\n\\paragraph{The memo.} Whether a state with $b(S)\\le k$ has a solution depends\non the state alone, not on the path that reached it~\\leanref{memo}. One\nconcern is that the memo and the old move might not combine: a refutation\nobtained with inherited old moves seems to depend on the ancestors, and one\nimplementation of the search declines to run both for that reason. A node\ntaken on its own, with an old move that is not in fact refuted, can indeed\nrecord a solvable state~\\leanref{fakeold}. Theorem~\\ref{thm:runs}\n(Appendix~\\ref{app:machinery}) shows that this never happens in a real run,\nbecause every old move is a refutation completed earlier in the same run.\n\n\\subsection{The repaired search is sound}\n\\label{sec:theorem}\n\n\\paragraph{What a rule must satisfy.} At a state $S$ with old moves $Q$, the\ncost cut keeps the playable candidates $P$ and the filter keeps a sublist\n$L\\subseteq P$. The filter is \\emph{node-sound} at $(S,Q)$ if, whenever every\n$q\\in Q$ has $\\neg\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$, $\\Sol_k(S)$ implies $\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$ for\nsome $c\\in L$~\\leanref{nodesound}. In terms of coverings, a node is sound when\ntwo things hold. First, every link a rule cites is a true covering. Second,\nevery chain of links from a discarded candidate ends in $L$ or in $Q$. The\nsecond condition can fail through a cycle even when every link is true, which\nis why the order of the rules matters. Proposition~\\ref{prop:filter} shows that\nthe repaired filter is node-sound, and Theorem~\\ref{thm:runs} that node\nsoundness, the memo and the old move together give sound runs; both are in\nAppendix~\\ref{app:machinery}. Together they give the main theorem.\n\n\\begin{theorem}[the revised search is sound]\\label{thm:main}\nRun the customer search with free moves, the memo, old moves and the filter\ndefinite $\\to$ subset $\\to$ better, the definite move firing only on a\nhereditarily definite customer and the better move citing only under the\nrepaired premise. If it refutes $k$ from the root, then no closing order costs\nat most $k$, $\\pw(G)>k-1$, and on the MOSP graph of an instance $M$ with at\nleast one 1, $Z(M)>k$~\\leanref{main}.\n\\end{theorem}\n\nIn words: each rule throws away only moves that a kept move is proved to be at\nleast as good as, and the memo and the old moves remember only failures that\nreally happened earlier in the same search. So when the search says ``no'',\nno closing order fits in $k$ stacks.\n\n\\paragraph{What is proved and what is tested.} The theorem is about the search\nas specified; that our C and Python implementations run it is checked, not\nproved. Three checks link them:\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item a transcription of the Lean filter agreed with the implemented filter on\n  16,244,090 node checks over graphs on 1 to 17 vertices;\n\\item the matching agreed with the hereditary premise on 36,939,226 candidates;\n\\item a search written from the theorems agreed with both implementations and\n  an exact oracle on 17,431,232 runs over 52,592 graphs on 1 to 17 vertices.\n\\end{itemize}\nSection~\\ref{sec:results} adds a certificate checker for individual\nrefutations.\n\n\\paragraph{The published rules.} Under the published rules the filter is not\nnode-sound~\\leanref{notsound}. In a check of 570,206 runs with memo and old\nmove, it lost the last solution at a node in 58 runs, all on graphs built\naround Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} and all at $k$ equal to the optimum.\nEvery one found a solution through another branch and answered correctly. No\nwhole instance on which the published rules answer \\emph{unsat} wrongly is\nknown.\n\n\\subsection{The rules in pathwidth language}\n\\label{sec:layouts}\n\nThe definite move has an exact counterpart in the pathwidth literature, and\nthis subsection makes the correspondence precise.\n\\citet[p.~142]{Kitsunai2016}, stating Tamaki's notion, call an extension $\\tau$\nof a vertex sequence $\\sigma$ \\emph{committable} when\n$|\\partial X'|\\ge|\\partial V(\\tau)|$ for every $X'$ with\n$V(\\sigma)\\subseteq X'\\subseteq V(\\tau)$. Their Lemma~1 says that if $\\sigma$\nextends to a feasible permutation, so does $\\tau$.\n\n\\begin{lemma}\\label{lem:endpoint}\nFor $q\\notin S$ and $X=\\cl(S\\cup\\{q\\})$: the published premise holds iff\n$b(X)\\le b(S)$, the commitment condition at the single set $X'=S$; and the\nrepaired premise holds iff $X$ is a commitment from $S$, the condition at every\n$X'$ between them~\\leanref{endpoint}.\n\\end{lemma}\n\nSo Theorem~\\ref{thm:repaired} is the commitment lemma at the child, and the\npublished definite move is a commitment whose interior condition was dropped.\nIn Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} the endpoint test passes with\n$b(X)=3=b(S)$, while the interior set $\\{2,3,4\\}$ has border\n2~\\leanref{cexnotcommit}. The published premise is not wasted: by\n\\citet[Lemma~10, Cor.~2, pp.~148--149]{Kitsunai2016} it guarantees a\ncommitment to the least-border set between $S$ and $X$, which need not contain\n$q$~\\leanref{existscommit}. In the counterexample that set is\n$\\{2,3,4\\}$~\\leanref{commit234}. Committing there when the matching fails is a\nsecond repair that gives up no pruning; we have not built it.\n\nA commitment of depth one, $S$ to $S\\cup\\{q\\}$, holds iff\n$\\open(q,S)\\le1$~\\leanref{depth1}. The greedy step of\n\\citet[Lemma~3, p.~49]{CoudertMazauricNisse2014} is exactly that\ncondition~\\leanref{greedy}, and the push rule of\n\\citet[Rule~1, p.~330]{SuchanVillanger2009} is a case of it. So every published\npathwidth rule of this kind lies in the region $\\open\\le1$, where the published\ndefinite move is right; \\citet[p.~388, Table~2]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014}\nreport depth-one commitments as ``extremely effective''. Chu and Stuckey's\ndefinite move reaches into $\\open\\ge2$, and that is where it fails. For the\ncost of the test, \\citet[Cor.~2]{Kitsunai2016} find the least border between\nthe ends of a given extension by a minimum $s$--$t$ separator;\nTheorem~\\ref{thm:matching} is that computation for the single target $X$, where\nit becomes a bipartite matching.\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:known} sums up, and its rows are grouped by what they say. The\npublished definite and better moves are false. The free move and the repaired\ndefinite move are known pathwidth results. The subset rule, the repaired\nbetter move and the old move have no counterpart we found, and are proved\nhere, as is the matching test. The comparison is limited to the pathwidth\npapers we hold\n\\citep{SuchanVillanger2009,Bodlaender2012,CoudertMazauricNisse2014,CoudertMazauricNisse2016,KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014,Kitsunai2016};\n``none found'' is not a claim of priority.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{Two published rules are false, two repaired or reused rules are known\npathwidth results, and the rest, with the matching test, are proved here: each\nrule of the customer search against the exact-pathwidth literature.}\n\\label{tab:known}\n\\begin{tabularx}{\\linewidth}{@{}>{\\raggedright}p{3.2cm}>{\\raggedright}X>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}p{4.1cm}@{}}\n\\toprule\nRule & Published counterpart & Status \\\\\n\\midrule\ndefinite move, published & -- & false (Cex.~\\ref{cex:definite}) \\\\\nbetter move, published & none found & false \\\\\n\\midrule\nfree move & full sets \\citep{SuchanVillanger2009}; \\citet[Prop.~3]{Kitsunai2016} & known \\\\\ndefinite move, $\\open\\le1$ & depth-one commitments; greedy step; push rule & known \\\\\ndefinite move, repaired & commitment lemma \\citep[Lemma~1]{Kitsunai2016} & known; matching test (Thm~\\ref{thm:matching}) not found \\\\\nmemo & subset DP \\citep{Bodlaender2012}; prefix and failure tables & known; with the old move, proved here (Thm~\\ref{thm:runs}) \\\\\n\\midrule\nsubset rule & none found & proved here \\\\\nbetter move, repaired & none found & proved here \\\\\nold move & none found & proved here \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabularx}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Prior reports.} We found no earlier report of the error, in 56 of\nthe 141 works that cite the customer search, read in full\n(Appendix~\\ref{app:prior}). The pathwidth literature has the correct form of\nthe rule, as a commitment, and none of the pathwidth papers we hold mentions\nMOSP.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":111,"newStart":1,"newLines":245,"lines":["-% Section 4. Sources: paper2/revised_algorithm.md (4.1-4.5), search_soundness.md,","+% Section 4. Sources: paper2/revised_algorithm.md (4.1-4.6), search_soundness.md,"," % prior_art_counterexample.md (prior art). Lean: lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/,"," % names in Appendix A (sec9a_appendix_lean.tex). The old 4.6-4.7 are Section 5.","+% Restructured 2026-10-06 (loop0009 item 05, review changes 4 and 5): stakes","+% first, the pathwidth reading and the dictionary in 4.1, Algorithm 4.1 in 4.2,","+% each rule as intuition -> counterexample -> repair -> test."," % Page numbers for Chu & Stuckey (2009) are those of the preprint"," % literature/chu_stuckey_2009.pdf; for Chu (2011) the printed ones.","-\\section{An exact open-stacks search, read as a pathwidth solver}","+\\section{An exact open-stacks search, read as a pathwidth algorithm}"," \\label{sec:search}"," ","-The best exact method for MOSP on the standard benchmarks is the \\emph{customer","-search} of \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}. It searches over the orders in which the","-customers' stacks close, not over product sequences, and prunes with dominance","-rules, three of them stated as theorems (their Theorems~1, 2 and~3) and a fourth,","-the subset rule, in their \\S2. \\citet{ChuStuckey2009} place MOSP among ``graph","-path-width and gate matrix layout'', citing \\citet{LY2002}, but do not use","-pathwidth in the search. By Theorem~\\ref{thm:mosp} it is nevertheless a","-pathwidth algorithm: any graph is the MOSP graph of the instance with one","-customer per vertex and one product per edge, whose optimum is pathwidth plus","-one.","+If MOSP is pathwidth (Theorem~\\ref{thm:mosp}), then the best exact MOSP","+algorithm is a pathwidth algorithm, and its rules can be checked against what","+the pathwidth literature knows. This section does that for the \\emph{customer","+search} of \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}, the search that Section~\\ref{sec:names}","+traced from the open-stacks challenge. It searches over the orders in which the","+customers' stacks close, not over pattern sequences, and it prunes with","+dominance rules. Three of the rules are stated as theorems (their Theorems~1,","+2 and~3) and a fourth, the subset rule, in their \\S2. \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}","+place MOSP among ``graph path-width and gate matrix layout'', citing","+\\citet{LY2002}, but they do not use pathwidth in the search. Any graph is the","+MOSP graph of the instance with one customer per vertex and one pattern per","+edge, so the search decides pathwidth for every graph."," ","-This section reads the search that way, and finds three things. Two of the","-published theorems are false, and so are their restatements in Chu's thesis","-\\citep{Chu2011} and, with a stronger premise, in \\citet{Fink2012}. The repaired","-first rule is an instance of a known pathwidth theorem, Tamaki's commitment","-lemma; the published rule is that lemma with its interior condition dropped. And","-the whole repaired search is proved sound in Lean.","+The stakes are these. Two of the published dominance theorems, the","+\\emph{definite move} and the \\emph{better move}, are false, and so are their","+restatements in Chu's thesis \\citep{Chu2011} and, with a stronger premise, in","+\\citet{Fink2012}. Yet no wrong answer is known: no whole instance on which the","+published rules answer ``no'' wrongly has been found, and when every benchmark","+optimum that rested on the search was proved again with repaired rules, no","+value changed (Section~\\ref{sec:results}). The correct form of the definite","+move was already known for pathwidth, as Tamaki's commitment lemma, and we","+show that it can be tested by one small bipartite matching per candidate. The","+main result, Theorem~\\ref{thm:main}, says informally: \\emph{with both rules","+repaired, the search is sound; whenever it answers that $k$ stacks do not","+suffice, they do not, and the graph has pathwidth at least $k$.} The repair","+costs at most 2\\% more search nodes on each benchmark set","+(Section~\\ref{sec:results})."," ","+Section~\\ref{sec:search-def} defines the search and shows that it decides","+pathwidth, in both vocabularies. Section~\\ref{sec:algorithm} gives the","+algorithm. Sections~\\ref{sec:definite} and~\\ref{sec:other-rules} take the","+rules one at a time, Section~\\ref{sec:theorem} proves the repaired search","+sound, and Section~\\ref{sec:layouts} places each rule in the pathwidth","+literature.","+"," \\subsection{The search decides pathwidth}"," \\label{sec:search-def}"," ","-\\citet[p.~4]{ChuStuckey2009} observe that the products matter only through the","-MOSP graph. So fix a graph $G=(C,E)$ on the customers and an integer $k\\ge0$.","-For a customer $c$ and a set $T\\subseteq C$ of closed customers, write","-$N[c]$ for the closed neighbourhood of $c$, and","+\\citet[p.~4]{ChuStuckey2009} observe that the patterns matter only through the","+MOSP graph. So fix a graph $G=(C,E)$ whose vertices are the customers, and an","+integer $k\\ge0$. In this section $k$ bounds the number of open stacks, which is","+one more than a width: $k$ stacks suffice exactly when the pathwidth is at most","+$k-1$ (Theorem~\\ref{thm:decides}). For a customer $c$ write $N[c]$ for its","+closed neighbourhood, the stacks that must be open when $c$'s stack closes. For","+a set $T\\subseteq C$ of vertices, its \\emph{border} $\\partial T$ is the set of","+vertices outside $T$ with a neighbour in $T$. For a set $T$ of closed","+customers write"," \\begin{align*}"," O(T) &= \\textstyle\\bigcup_{c\\in T}N[c] && \\text{the stacks opened once $T$ has closed},\\\\"," \\fin(X) &= \\{c : N[c]\\subseteq X\\} && \\text{the customers free to close when $X$ is open},\\\\"," \\cl(T) &= \\fin(O(T)) && \\text{the \\emph{free closure} of $T$},\\\\","-b(T) &= |O(T)\\setminus T| && \\text{the stacks open after $T$ has closed},\\\\","+b(T) &= |O(T)\\setminus T| = |\\partial T| && \\text{the stacks open after $T$ has closed},\\\\"," o(c,T) &= N[c]\\setminus O(T),\\quad \\open(c,T)=|o(c,T)| && \\text{the stacks closing $c$ would newly open.}"," \\end{align*}","+The open stacks after $T$ has closed are exactly the border of $T$, and this","+one identity carries the whole translation to pathwidth.","+"," A \\emph{move} at $T$ closes one customer $c\\notin T$, at cost"," $\\cost(T,c)=|O(T\\cup\\{c\\})\\setminus T| = b(T\\cup\\{c\\})+1$, the stacks open at","-that moment counting $c$; it is \\emph{playable} when $\\cost(T,c)\\le k$. Write","+that moment counting $c$. It is \\emph{playable} when $\\cost(T,c)\\le k$. Write"," $P_k(T)$ when some order of $C\\setminus T$ has every step playable. On entering"," a node the search closes every free customer, so it stands only at","-\\emph{states}, sets with $S=\\cl(S)$, and the \\emph{child} of $S$ by $c$ is","-$S\\cdot c=\\cl(S\\cup\\{c\\})$. The search's predicate $\\Sol_k(S)$ holds when $S=C$,","-or when some playable $c$ has $\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$~\\leanref{defs}.","-Every node the search visits satisfies the invariant $b(S)\\le k$.","+\\emph{states}, sets with $S=\\cl(S)$. The \\emph{child} of $S$ by $c$ is","+$S\\cdot c=\\cl(S\\cup\\{c\\})$. The search's predicate $\\Sol_k(S)$ holds when","+$S=C$, or when some playable $c$ has $\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$~\\leanref{defs}. Every","+node the search visits satisfies the invariant $b(S)\\le k$."," ","+\\paragraph{An example.} Take the graph of Figure~\\ref{fig:example}(d), with","+$k=3$. At $T=\\{a\\}$ we have $O(T)=\\{a,b\\}$, so one stack, $b$, is open:","+$b(T)=1$ and $\\partial T=\\{b\\}$. No other customer is free, since $N[b]$","+contains $c$ and $d$, so $T$ is a state. Closing $b$ newly opens","+$o(b,T)=\\{c,d\\}$ and costs $\\cost(T,b)=3$, the stacks $b$, $c$ and $d$; the","+move is playable. At $\\{a,b,c\\}$ every neighbour of $d$ is open, so $d$ is","+free, and the child $\\{a,b\\}\\cdot c$ is $\\{a,b,c,d\\}$. The closing order","+$a,b,c,e$ (with $d$ and $f$ closed free) costs $2,3,3,2$, the profile of","+order~A in Figure~\\ref{fig:example}(a).","+"," \\begin{lemma}[free moves never hurt]\\label{lem:F}"," If $b(T)\\le k$, then $P_k(T)\\iff\\Sol_k(\\cl(T))$~\\leanref{F}."," \\end{lemma}","-Closing a customer whose neighbourhood is open opens nothing, never makes a","-later step dearer, and leaves one fewer stack open. The hypothesis is needed:","-closing the free customers one by one costs $b(T)$ at each step.","+Closing a customer whose neighbourhood is already open opens nothing, never","+makes a later step dearer, and leaves one fewer stack open. The hypothesis is","+needed: closing the free customers one by one costs $b(T)$ at each step."," "," \\begin{theorem}[the search decides pathwidth]\\label{thm:decides}","-For nonempty $C$, $\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)\\iff\\pw(G)+1\\le k$; on the MOSP graph of an","-instance $M$ with a requirement, $\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)\\iff Z(M)\\le k$~\\leanref{decides}.","+For nonempty $C$, $\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)\\iff\\pw(G)+1\\le k$. On the MOSP graph of an","+instance $M$ with at least one 1, $\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)\\iff Z(M)\\le k$~\\leanref{decides}."," \\end{theorem}"," \\begin{proof}"," For a closing order $c_1,\\dots,c_n$ with prefixes $S_i$, the $i$-th step costs","-$1+|\\partial S_i|$, where $\\partial X$ is the set of vertices outside $X$ with a","-neighbour in $X$. Read backwards as a layout, the cut separating $C\\setminus S_i$","-from $S_i$ counts exactly $\\partial S_i$. So the cost of the order is the vertex","-separation of the reversed layout plus one, and Lemma~\\ref{lem:F} at the root and","-Theorem~\\ref{thm:vs} finish the proof. The Lean statement goes through","-narrowness \\citep{LY2002ref11}, which counts the forward order directly.","+$1+|\\partial S_i|$. Reverse the order to get a layout. Its first $n-i$","+vertices are $C\\setminus S_i$, and those with a neighbour among the remaining","+ones are exactly $\\partial S_i$. So the cost of the closing order is the vertex","+separation of the reversed layout plus one, and Lemma~\\ref{lem:F} at the root","+and Theorem~\\ref{thm:vs} finish the proof."," \\end{proof}"," "," So a refutation, $\\neg\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)$, may only mean $\\pw(G)>k-1$. The rest"," of the section asks whether the pruning rules preserve that meaning."," ","-\\paragraph{What a rule must satisfy.} At a state $S$ the search has a set $Q$ of","-\\emph{old moves} (Section~\\ref{sec:other-rules}). Its candidates are the","-customers outside $S\\cup Q$; the \\emph{cost cut} keeps the playable ones, $P$,","-in index order, and a \\emph{filter} keeps a sublist $L\\subseteq P$. The filter","-is \\emph{node-sound} at $(S,Q)$ if, whenever every $q\\in Q$ has","-$\\neg\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$, $\\Sol_k(S)$ implies $\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$ for some $c\\in L$~\\leanref{nodesound}.","-Each rule justifies a discarded move $r$ by a \\emph{covering}, a move $c$ with","-$\\Sol_k(S\\cdot r)\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$. A node is sound when every link a","-rule cites is a true covering and every chain of links from a discarded","-candidate ends in $L$ or in $Q$; the second condition can fail through a cycle","-even when every link is true. The filter runs the definite move, then the subset","-rule, then the better move, each once.","+\\paragraph{In pathwidth language.} The exact pathwidth literature writes","+$N(T)$ for the border and $d(T)$ for its size","+\\citep[p.~390]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014}; we keep $\\partial T$ and $b(T)$. A","+vertex sequence is \\emph{$w$-feasible} when every prefix has border at most","+$w$, and $\\vs(G)$ is the least $w$ for which some permutation is $w$-feasible.","+Table~\\ref{tab:dictionary} translates each notion of the search into this","+vocabulary. Read it row by row: every ingredient of the customer search is a","+known ingredient of an exact pathwidth algorithm, and the last two rows are","+the rules of Section~\\ref{sec:algorithm}."," ","-\\subsection{The definite move is false as published}","+\\begin{table}[t]","+\\centering\\small","+\\caption{The customer search is, ingredient by ingredient, an exact pathwidth","+algorithm: each notion of the search beside its name in the pathwidth","+literature. The memo and the definite move are described in","+Section~\\ref{sec:algorithm}.}","+\\label{tab:dictionary}","+\\begin{tabularx}{\\linewidth}{@{}>{\\raggedright}p{5.2cm}>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X@{}}","+\\toprule","+Customer search & Layout \\\\","+\\midrule","+closing order from $\\emptyset$ & vertex sequence, read forwards \\\\","+open stacks $b(T)$ & border size $|\\partial T|$ \\\\","+$\\cost(T,c)$ & $|\\partial(T\\cup\\{c\\})|+1$ \\\\","+$\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)$ & $\\vs(G)\\le k-1$ \\\\","+free closure $\\cl(T)$ & full set of $T$ \\citep[p.~328]{SuchanVillanger2009} \\\\","+memo & failure table \\\\","+definite move, repaired & commitment to the child \\\\","+\\bottomrule\\end{tabularx}","+\\end{table}","+","+\\subsection{The algorithm}","+\\label{sec:algorithm}","+","+Algorithm~\\ref{alg:search} gives the customer search. It is a depth-first","+search over states. At each node it closes the free customers, consults a","+table of states already refuted, keeps the playable candidates, filters them","+with the dominance rules and recurses on the survivors.","+","+\\begin{algorithm}[t]","+\\caption{The customer search \\citep{ChuStuckey2009}. It decides $\\Sol_k(S)$","+for a graph $G=(C,E)$ and a stack bound $k$, and is called as","+$\\textsc{Search}(\\emptyset,\\emptyset)$. $Q$ is the set of old moves the node","+inherits.}","+\\label{alg:search}","+\\small","+\\begin{tabular}{@{}r@{\\quad}l@{\\qquad}l@{}}","+1 & $\\textsc{Search}(S,Q)$: & \\\\","+2 & \\quad $S\\gets\\cl(S)$ & \\textcolor{gray}{free moves (Lemma~\\ref{lem:F})} \\\\","+3 & \\quad \\textbf{if} $S=C$ \\textbf{then return} true & \\\\","+4 & \\quad \\textbf{if} $S$ is in the memo \\textbf{then return} false & \\textcolor{gray}{memo} \\\\","+5 & \\quad $P\\gets$ the $c\\notin S\\cup Q$ with $\\cost(S,c)\\le k$, in index order & \\textcolor{gray}{cost cut} \\\\","+6 & \\quad $L\\gets$ filter of $P$: definite move, then subset rule, then better move & \\textcolor{gray}{Sections~\\ref{sec:definite}--\\ref{sec:other-rules}} \\\\","+7 & \\quad $\\mathit{seen}\\gets Q\\setminus S$ & \\\\","+8 & \\quad \\textbf{for each} $c\\in L$: & \\\\","+9 & \\quad\\quad $Q'\\gets\\{q\\in\\mathit{seen} : \\cost(S\\cup\\{q\\},c)\\le k\\}$ & \\textcolor{gray}{old moves} \\\\","+10 & \\quad\\quad \\textbf{if} $\\textsc{Search}(S\\cup\\{c\\},Q')$ \\textbf{then return} true & \\\\","+11 & \\quad\\quad $\\mathit{seen}\\gets\\mathit{seen}\\cup\\{c\\}$ & \\\\","+12 & \\quad add $S$ to the memo & \\\\","+13 & \\quad \\textbf{return} false & \\\\","+\\end{tabular}","+\\end{algorithm}","+","+Line~2 is safe by Lemma~\\ref{lem:F}, and the cost cut of line~5 is exact,","+since an unplayable move is not a move of $\\Sol_k$. The rest is pruning, and","+each piece has a one-sentence intuition.","+\\begin{itemize}","+\\item \\emph{The memo} (lines~4 and~12): whether a state can be finished does","+  not depend on how it was reached, so a state refuted once is refused when","+  met again.","+\\item \\emph{Old moves} (lines~7--11): a sibling whose subtree failed stays","+  failed further down, as long as closing it first would have kept each later","+  step within the bound.","+\\item \\emph{The definite move}: if closing $q$ now leaves no more stacks open","+  than before, it seems safe to close $q$ at once and try nothing else.","+\\item \\emph{The subset rule}: if $d$ newly opens a subset of what $r$ newly","+  opens, then $d$ is at least as good a move as $r$.","+\\item \\emph{The better move}: if $q$ would be a definite move right after $r$,","+  then closing $q$ before $r$ is at least as good as closing $r$ first.","+\\end{itemize}","+Each of the three filtering rules discards a move $r$ by citing a","+\\emph{covering}, a kept move $c$ with","+$\\Sol_k(S\\cdot r)\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$. The filter runs the definite","+move, then the subset rule, then the better move, each once. If the definite","+move fires, it keeps its customer alone.","+","+This loop is the decision procedure of \\citet[Algorithm~1,","+p.~393]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014}, which asks whether a vertex set extends to","+a $w$-feasible permutation: replace the set by a committed extension, answer","+from a failure table or at the full vertex set, recurse on each feasible","+one-vertex extension, and record failures. The customer search is that loop","+with $w=k-1$. The free closure and the definite move play the part of the","+commitment, the memo is the failure table, and the cost cut is the feasibility","+test. \\citet[\\S3.2]{CoudertMazauricNisse2014} has the same three parts in a","+branch and bound. Neither line cites the other in the papers we hold:","+\\citet{ChuStuckey2009} predates the commitments and both 2014 papers, and none","+of the pathwidth papers cites it. This is the isolation measured in","+Table~\\ref{tab:communities}, at the level of one algorithm.","+","+\\subsection{The definite move is false as published, and its repair is a matching}"," \\label{sec:definite}"," ","-\\citet[\\S3.1, p.~6]{ChuStuckey2009} state:","+The definite move is the strongest of the rules, since when it fires it","+discards every other candidate. Its premise looks like a safe accounting:","+closing $q$ opens some new stacks and lets some customers close, and if it","+lets at least as many close as it opens, nothing seems lost by closing it","+first. \\citet[\\S3.1, p.~6]{ChuStuckey2009} state:"," \\begin{quote}"," \\textbf{Theorem 1.} Suppose S ++ [q] is playable and close(q, S) $\\ge$"," open(q, S), then if U$'$ = S ++ R is a solution, there exists a solution","-U = S ++ [q] ++ R$'$,","+U = S ++ [q] ++ R$'$."," \\end{quote}","-where $\\close(q,S)$ counts the customers $d$ with $o(d,S)\\subseteq o(q,S)$","-(p.~4). The rule keeps $q$ alone. We read $d$ as ranging over customers not yet","-closed, as the code does; read literally, closed customers count too, which","-weakens the premise, and both readings are refuted below. \\citet[Thm~6.3.6,","-p.~142]{Chu2011} restates the theorem with the same premise and proof, reworded","-for $k$ stacks. Write $X=S\\cdot q$.","+Here ++ is concatenation of closing sequences, a \\emph{solution} is a complete","+closing order with every step playable, and $\\close(q,S)$ counts the customers","+$d$ with $o(d,S)\\subseteq o(q,S)$ (p.~4). The rule keeps $q$ alone. We read $d$","+as ranging over customers not yet closed, as our implementation does; read","+literally, closed customers count too, which weakens the premise, and both","+readings are refuted below. \\citet[Thm~6.3.6, p.~142]{Chu2011} restates the","+theorem with the same premise and proof, reworded for $k$ stacks. Write","+$X=S\\cdot q$. The first lemma confirms that the premise says what the","+intuition says."," "," \\begin{lemma}[what the premise says]\\label{lem:premise}"," $\\close(q,S)=|X\\setminus S|$. Hence the premise holds iff $b(X)\\le b(S)$: the"," child has no more open stacks than the parent~\\leanref{premise}."," \\end{lemma}"," ","+The premise compares only the two ends, $S$ and $X$. The rule fails because a","+set between them can be cheaper still. Figure~\\ref{fig:cex} shows the graph on","+which it fails.","+"," \\begin{figure}[t]"," \\centering"," \\includegraphics[width=0.85\\linewidth]{definite_counterexample.pdf}"]},{"oldStart":119,"oldLines":35,"newStart":253,"newLines":41,"lines":[" \\end{figure}"," "," \\begin{counterexample}\\label{cex:definite}","-Let $G$ be the graph on customers $0,\\dots,13$ with the 26 edges","+Let $G$ be the graph of Figure~\\ref{fig:cex}, on customers $0,\\dots,13$ with","+the 26 edges"," \\begin{gather*}"," 0\\text{--}3,\\ 0\\text{--}4,\\ 0\\text{--}7,\\ 0\\text{--}11,\\ 1\\text{--}2,\\ 1\\text{--}5,\\ 1\\text{--}6,\\ 2\\text{--}3,\\ 2\\text{--}4,\\ 5\\text{--}9,\\ 5\\text{--}11,\\ 5\\text{--}12,\\ 6\\text{--}8,\\\\"," 6\\text{--}13,\\ 7\\text{--}9,\\ 7\\text{--}10,\\ 7\\text{--}13,\\ 8\\text{--}9,\\ 8\\text{--}10,\\ 8\\text{--}11,\\ 9\\text{--}10,\\ 9\\text{--}11,\\ 10\\text{--}11,\\ 10\\text{--}12,\\ 10\\text{--}13,\\ 12\\text{--}13,"," \\end{gather*}","-with $S=\\{2\\}$, $k=6$ and $q=0$ (Figure~\\ref{fig:cex}). Then $O(S)=\\{1,2,3,4\\}$,","-$o(0,S)=\\{0,7,11\\}$ and $\\cost(S,0)=6$, so $0$ is playable; the customers with","-$o(d,S)\\subseteq\\{0,7,11\\}$ are $0$, $3$ and $4$, so $\\close(0,S)=3\\ge\\open(0,S)$;","+with $S=\\{2\\}$, $k=6$ and $q=0$. Then $O(S)=\\{1,2,3,4\\}$,","+$o(0,S)=\\{0,7,11\\}$ and $\\cost(S,0)=6$, so $0$ is playable. The customers with","+$o(d,S)\\subseteq\\{0,7,11\\}$ are $0$, $3$ and $4$, so $\\close(0,S)=3\\ge\\open(0,S)$,"," and $0$ comes first in index order, so the rule keeps only $0$. A solution"," exists from $S$: the order"," \\[1,3,4,6,12,13,0,5,7,8,9,10,11\\]","-costs at most 6. None exists","-from the child $X=\\{0,2,3,4\\}$: only seven sets are reachable from $X$ by moves","-of cost at most 6, and none is $C$~\\leanref{cex}.","+costs at most 6. None exists from the child $X=\\{0,2,3,4\\}$: only seven sets","+are reachable from $X$ by moves of cost at most 6, and none is $C$~\\leanref{cex}."," \\end{counterexample}"," "," The published implication, as a universal statement, is refuted in Lean under"," both readings of $\\close$, and so is the thesis's Theorem~6.3.6~\\leanref{thm1false}.","-Appendix~\\ref{app:minimality} gives the search for smaller counterexamples, and","-Appendix~\\ref{app:whyfails} the analysis of why the published proof fails.","-\\citet[pp.~27--28]{Fink2012} restates the rule with a","-stronger premise, counting only dominated customers of smaller index than $q$.","-The graph above refutes it under no labelling, but a third twin of 3 and 4 gives","-a 15-customer graph that does~\\leanref{fink}.","+\\citet[pp.~27--28]{Fink2012} restates the rule with a stronger premise,","+counting only dominated customers of smaller index than $q$. The graph above","+refutes it under no labelling, but a third twin of 3 and 4 gives a","+15-customer graph that does~\\leanref{fink}. The published proof moves $q$ to","+the front of a solution and counts, after each prefix, the stacks $q$ opens","+against the customers it lets close. But customers that the solution has","+already closed no longer count as closed by $q$, while the stacks $q$ opens may","+still be new. Appendix~\\ref{app:whyfails} gives the full analysis, and","+Appendix~\\ref{app:minimality} the search for smaller counterexamples."," ","-\\paragraph{The repair.} Call $q$ \\emph{hereditarily definite} at $S$ when","-$b(X)\\le b(B)$ for every $B$ with $S\\subseteq B\\subseteq X$ and $q\\notin B$. By","-Lemma~\\ref{lem:premise}, the published premise is the case $B=S$. In the","-counterexample the repair fails at $B=\\{2,3,4\\}$, where $b(B)=2<3=b(X)$~\\leanref{hered}.","+\\paragraph{The repair.} The fix is to require the comparison at every set","+between the two ends, not only at $S$. Call $q$ \\emph{hereditarily definite} at","+$S$ when $b(X)\\le b(B)$ for every $B$ with $S\\subseteq B\\subseteq X$ and","+$q\\notin B$. By Lemma~\\ref{lem:premise}, the published premise is the case","+$B=S$. In the counterexample the repair fails at $B=\\{2,3,4\\}$, where","+$b(B)=2<3=b(X)$~\\leanref{hered}. The proof that the repair is sound needs one","+inequality."," "," \\begin{lemma}[open stacks are submodular]\\label{lem:submod}"," For all $A,B\\subseteq C$, $b(A\\cup B)+b(A\\cap B)\\le b(A)+b(B)$~\\leanref{submod}."]},{"oldStart":164,"oldLines":20,"newStart":304,"newLines":27,"lines":[" \\begin{proof}"," Take an order from $S$ of cost at most $k$ with prefixes $T_i$, in which $q$ is"," the $j$-th step, and follow it from $X$, skipping customers already in $X$. A","-step $i<j$ closes $c_i$ at $T_{i-1}\\cup X$. With $Z=T_i$ we have","-$S\\subseteq Z\\cap X\\subseteq X$ and $q\\notin Z$, so the hypothesis gives","-$b(X)\\le b(Z\\cap X)$, and Lemma~\\ref{lem:submod} gives $b(Z\\cup X)\\le b(Z)$: the","-step costs no more than the original. After step $j-1$ the closed set contains","-$T_j$ with the same opened set, and the rest of the order applies. The search's","-form follows by Lemma~\\ref{lem:F}.","+step $i<j$ closes $c_i$ at $T_{i-1}\\cup X$. We have","+$S\\subseteq T_i\\cap X\\subseteq X$ and $q\\notin T_i$, so the hypothesis gives","+$b(X)\\le b(T_i\\cap X)$, and Lemma~\\ref{lem:submod} gives","+$b(T_i\\cup X)\\le b(T_i)$: the step costs no more than the original. After step","+$j-1$ the closed set contains $T_j$ with the same opened set, and the rest of","+the order applies. The search's form follows by Lemma~\\ref{lem:F}."," \\end{proof}"," "," This theorem is not new. The hereditary premise is the commitment condition of"," Tamaki, and the proof is the proof of the commitment lemma,"," \\citet[Lemma~1, p.~142]{Kitsunai2016}; Section~\\ref{sec:layouts} makes the","-correspondence exact. What we did not find in the papers we hold is the","-following cheap form of the test.","+correspondence exact."," ","+\\paragraph{The matching test.} The repaired rule seems to require checking","+exponentially many intermediate sets. It does not, and this cheap test is the","+algorithmic contribution of the section; we did not find it in the papers we","+hold. In words: the rule is safe exactly when all but one of the new stacks","+can be paired with a different freed customer who needed it. In the","+counterexample, closing 0 opens three new stacks and frees 3 and 4, both of","+whom needed only stack 0: one pairing where two are required.","+"," \\begin{theorem}[the repair is a matching condition]\\label{thm:matching}"," Let $Y=X\\setminus(S\\cup\\{q\\})$, and join each $d\\in Y$ to the stacks of"," $o(d,S)\\subseteq o(q,S)$. Then $q$ is hereditarily definite at $S$ iff this"]},{"oldStart":189,"oldLines":217,"newStart":336,"newLines":219,"lines":[" $\\delta=|Y|+1-\\open(q,S)$, where $o(D)$ is the union of the $o(d,S)$. By the"," deficiency form of Hall's theorem the largest matching has size"," $|Y|-\\max_D(|D|-|o(D)|)$, which is at least $|Y|-\\delta=\\open(q,S)-1$ exactly","-when the repair holds. The Lean proof reduces the deficiency form to Mathlib's","-Hall theorem with $\\delta$ dummy stacks.","+when the repair holds."," \\end{proof}"," ","-In words: the rule is safe exactly when all but one of the new stacks can be","-paired with a different freed customer who needed it. In the counterexample,","-closing 0 opens three new stacks and frees 3 and 4, both of whom needed only","-stack 0: one pairing where two are required. Every $q$ with $\\open(q,S)\\le1$","-passes at once, so the published rule is right there~\\leanref{open1}.","+The bipartite graph has at most $\\close-1$ customers on one side and $\\open$","+stacks on the other, and the matching can stop at $\\open-1$ edges. Every $q$","+with $\\open(q,S)\\le1$ passes at once, so the published rule is right","+there~\\leanref{open1}."," "," \\subsection{The other rules}"," \\label{sec:other-rules}"," ","+Each of the remaining rules is quoted, proved sound in the form used, and","+shown to need the test or tie-break it carries.","+"," \\paragraph{The subset rule.} ``If $o(c_i,S)\\subseteq o(c_j,S)$ and $i<j$ \\dots\\","-move $j$ can be removed'' \\citep[\\S2, p.~5]{ChuStuckey2009}. As coded, $d$","-dominates $r$ when $o(d,S)\\subseteq o(r,S)$ and the inclusion is strict or $d<r$;","-every dominated playable $r$ is dropped, with a fallback that keeps $P$ if all","-would go. The covering is sound for every set $S$: if $o(d,S)\\subseteq o(r,S)$","-and $r$ is playable, then $d$ is playable and $\\Sol_k(S\\cdot r)\\Rightarrow","-\\Sol_k(S\\cdot d)$, because $d$ is free after $r$ and closing $d$ then $r$ reaches","-the same state at no greater cost. With the index tie-break, domination is a","-strict order, so an undominated move with a solution survives, and the fallback","-never fires when a solution exists~\\leanref{subset}.","-The tie-break is necessary: without it, two customers with equal new stacks","-dominate each other and both go, which loses a solution on a 7-customer","-graph~\\leanref{tiebreak}.","+move $j$ can be removed'' \\citep[\\S2, p.~5]{ChuStuckey2009}. As implemented,","+$d$ dominates $r$ when $o(d,S)\\subseteq o(r,S)$ and the inclusion is strict or","+$d<r$. Every dominated playable $r$ is dropped, with a fallback that keeps $P$","+if all would go. The covering is sound for every set $S$: if","+$o(d,S)\\subseteq o(r,S)$ and $r$ is playable, then $d$ is playable and","+$\\Sol_k(S\\cdot r)\\Rightarrow \\Sol_k(S\\cdot d)$, because $d$ is free after $r$","+and closing $d$ then $r$ reaches the same state at no greater cost. With the","+index tie-break, domination is a strict order, so an undominated move with a","+solution survives, and the fallback never fires when a solution","+exists~\\leanref{subset}. The tie-break is necessary: without it, two customers","+with equal new stacks dominate each other and both go, which loses a solution","+on a 7-customer graph~\\leanref{tiebreak}."," "," \\paragraph{The better move.} \\citet[\\S3.2, p.~6]{ChuStuckey2009}: ``Suppose"," S ++ [q] and S ++ [r, q] are playable and close(q, S $\\cup$ \\{r\\}) $\\ge$"," open(q, S $\\cup$ \\{r\\}) then if U$'$ = S ++ [r] ++ R is a solution there exists a","-solution U = S ++ [q] ++ R$'$.'' Its premise is Theorem~1's premise for $q$ at the","-child $\\cl(S\\cup\\{r\\})$, together with playability of $r$ then $q$, and its","-conclusion swaps $q$ and $r$~\\leanref{isbetter}. The swap is sound; the appeal to","-Theorem~1 is not. On the graph of Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} at the root","-with $k=6$, the definite move does not fire, customers 0 and 2 both survive the","-subset rule, and the premise holds for $r=2$, $q=0$, so the rule drops 2 citing","-0. But $\\{2\\}$ has a solution and $\\{0\\}$ has none~\\leanref{bettercex}.","-At that node another kept customer still has a solution, so this is a false link","-and not a lost node; no node where the better move loses the last solution has","-been found. \\citet[Thm~6.3.8, p.~143]{Chu2011} states the better move with a","-different premise, $\\close(q,S)\\ge\\open(q,S\\cup\\{r\\})$, and proves it through","-Theorem~6.3.6. The same graph refutes it at $S=\\{2\\}$, $r=3$, $q=0$~\\leanref{thm638}.","-The repair cites $q$ for $r$ only if $q$ is hereditarily definite at","-$\\cl(S\\cup\\{r\\})$, one matching at the child; then the swap argument goes","+solution U = S ++ [q] ++ R$'$.'' Its premise is the definite move's premise for","+$q$ at the child $\\cl(S\\cup\\{r\\})$, together with playability of $r$ then $q$,","+and its conclusion swaps $q$ and $r$~\\leanref{isbetter}. The swap is sound,","+because both orders end at the same state and neither step overflows. The","+appeal to the definite move is not.","+","+\\emph{Counterexample.} On the graph of Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} at the","+root with $k=6$, the definite move does not fire, customers 0 and 2 both","+survive the subset rule, and the premise holds for $r=2$, $q=0$, so the rule","+drops 2 citing 0. But $\\{2\\}$ has a solution and $\\{0\\}$ has","+none~\\leanref{bettercex}. At that node another kept customer still has a","+solution, so this is a false link and not a lost node; no node where the","+better move loses the last solution has been found. \\citet[Thm~6.3.8,","+p.~143]{Chu2011} states the better move with a different premise,","+$\\close(q,S)\\ge\\open(q,S\\cup\\{r\\})$, and proves it through his Theorem~6.3.6.","+The same graph refutes it at $S=\\{2\\}$, $r=3$, $q=0$~\\leanref{thm638}.","+","+\\emph{Repair and test.} Cite $q$ for $r$ only if $q$ is hereditarily definite","+at $\\cl(S\\cup\\{r\\})$, which is one matching at the child","+(Theorem~\\ref{thm:matching}); then the swap argument goes"," through~\\leanref{repbetter}."," ","-Two earlier faults of this rule in our own implementation are now proved in","-Lean too: a close count that included the customers $r$ finishes on its own,","-and an order of the rules in which the subset rule could cite a customer the","-better move had already dropped, which loses a node through a cycle of true","-links~\\leanref{bugs}.","-The second is an instance of the general observation of","-\\citet[Prop.~3, Example~6]{Beck2025} that mutually dominating transitions must","-be tie-broken.","+\\emph{Two requirements.} The close count in the premise must not include the","+customers that $r$ finishes on its own; counting them makes the rule's own","+conclusion false on a 12-customer graph. And the rules must run in the order","+definite, subset, better, each citing only customers still standing: if the","+better move runs first, the subset rule can cite a customer the better move","+has already dropped, and an 8-customer graph loses a node through a cycle of","+links that are each true~\\leanref{bugs}. The second is an instance of the","+general observation of \\citet[Prop.~3, Example~6]{Beck2025} that mutually","+dominating transitions must be tie-broken."," ","-\\paragraph{The old move.} \\citet[Thm~3, p.~7]{ChuStuckey2009} let a child inherit","-a sibling $q$ whose subtree failed, provided that inserting $q$ before each","-later step keeps that step playable. The rule is sound as coded: if","+\\paragraph{The old move.} \\citet[Thm~3, p.~7]{ChuStuckey2009} let a child","+inherit a sibling $q$ whose subtree failed, provided that inserting $q$ before","+each later step keeps that step playable (line~9 of","+Algorithm~\\ref{alg:search}). The rule is sound as implemented: if"," $\\cost(S\\cup\\{q\\},c)\\le k$, then"," $\\Sol_k(\\cl(\\{q\\}\\cup S\\cdot c))\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$, so a refuted"," sibling stays refuted along any path whose steps pass the test~\\leanref{oldmove}."," The test is necessary; the smallest graph that shows it is the path on five"," vertices~\\leanref{reinsert}."," ","-\\paragraph{The memo.} A refuted state is recorded and refused when met again.","-Whether a state with $b(S)\\le k$ has a solution depends on the state alone, not","-on the path that reached it~\\leanref{memo}. One implementation of","-ours declined to run the memo beside the old move, on the ground that a","-refutation obtained with inherited old moves depends on the ancestors. A node","+\\paragraph{The memo.} Whether a state with $b(S)\\le k$ has a solution depends","+on the state alone, not on the path that reached it~\\leanref{memo}. One","+concern is that the memo and the old move might not combine: a refutation","+obtained with inherited old moves seems to depend on the ancestors, and one","+implementation of the search declines to run both for that reason. A node"," taken on its own, with an old move that is not in fact refuted, can indeed","-record a solvable state~\\leanref{fakeold}; Theorem~\\ref{thm:runs} (Appendix~\\ref{app:machinery}) shows that this","-never happens in a real run.","+record a solvable state~\\leanref{fakeold}. Theorem~\\ref{thm:runs}","+(Appendix~\\ref{app:machinery}) shows that this never happens in a real run,","+because every old move is a refutation completed earlier in the same run."," "," \\subsection{The repaired search is sound}"," \\label{sec:theorem}"," ","-The main theorem rests on two lemmas about the filter and about whole runs,","-Proposition~\\ref{prop:filter} and Theorem~\\ref{thm:runs}, which","-Appendix~\\ref{app:machinery} states and proves.","+\\paragraph{What a rule must satisfy.} At a state $S$ with old moves $Q$, the","+cost cut keeps the playable candidates $P$ and the filter keeps a sublist","+$L\\subseteq P$. The filter is \\emph{node-sound} at $(S,Q)$ if, whenever every","+$q\\in Q$ has $\\neg\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$, $\\Sol_k(S)$ implies $\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$ for","+some $c\\in L$~\\leanref{nodesound}. In terms of coverings, a node is sound when","+two things hold. First, every link a rule cites is a true covering. Second,","+every chain of links from a discarded candidate ends in $L$ or in $Q$. The","+second condition can fail through a cycle even when every link is true, which","+is why the order of the rules matters. Proposition~\\ref{prop:filter} shows that","+the repaired filter is node-sound, and Theorem~\\ref{thm:runs} that node","+soundness, the memo and the old move together give sound runs; both are in","+Appendix~\\ref{app:machinery}. Together they give the main theorem."," "," \\begin{theorem}[the revised search is sound]\\label{thm:main}"," Run the customer search with free moves, the memo, old moves and the filter"," definite $\\to$ subset $\\to$ better, the definite move firing only on a"," hereditarily definite customer and the better move citing only under the","-repaired premise. If it refutes $k$ from the root, then no closing order costs at","-most $k$, $\\pw(G)>k-1$, and on the MOSP graph of an instance $M$ with a","-requirement, $Z(M)>k$~\\leanref{main}.","+repaired premise. If it refutes $k$ from the root, then no closing order costs","+at most $k$, $\\pw(G)>k-1$, and on the MOSP graph of an instance $M$ with at","+least one 1, $Z(M)>k$~\\leanref{main}."," \\end{theorem}"," ","-What the Lean model covers is the search as specified; that our C and Python","-implement it is checked, not proved. A transcription of the Lean filter agreed","-with the production filter on 16,244,090 node checks over graphs on 1 to 17","-vertices; the matching agreed with the hereditary premise on 36,939,226","-candidates; and a search written from the theorems agreed with the C, the Python","-and an exact oracle on 17,431,232 runs over 52,592 graphs on 1 to 17","-vertices. Under the published rules the filter is not","-node-sound~\\leanref{notsound}, and in a check of 570,206 runs with","-memo and old move it lost the last solution at a node in 58 runs, all on graphs","-built around Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} and all at $k$ equal to the","-optimum. Every one found a solution through another branch and answered","-correctly. No whole instance on which the published rules answer \\emph{unsat}","-wrongly is known.","+In words: each rule throws away only moves that a kept move is proved to be at","+least as good as, and the memo and the old moves remember only failures that","+really happened earlier in the same search. So when the search says ``no'',","+no closing order fits in $k$ stacks."," ","-\\subsection{The search in pathwidth language}","-\\label{sec:layouts}","+\\paragraph{What is proved and what is tested.} The theorem is about the search","+as specified; that our C and Python implementations run it is checked, not","+proved. Three checks link them:","+\\begin{itemize}","+\\item a transcription of the Lean filter agreed with the implemented filter on","+  16,244,090 node checks over graphs on 1 to 17 vertices;","+\\item the matching agreed with the hereditary premise on 36,939,226 candidates;","+\\item a search written from the theorems agreed with both implementations and","+  an exact oracle on 17,431,232 runs over 52,592 graphs on 1 to 17 vertices.","+\\end{itemize}","+Section~\\ref{sec:results} adds a certificate checker for individual","+refutations."," ","-For a set $T$ of vertices write $N(T)$ for its \\emph{border}, the vertices","-outside $T$ with a neighbour inside, and $d(T)=|N(T)|$, in the notation of","-\\citet[p.~390]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014}. A vertex sequence is $w$-feasible when","-every prefix has $d\\le w$, and $\\vs(G)$ is the least $w$ for which some","-permutation is $w$-feasible. Table~\\ref{tab:dictionary} translates.","+\\paragraph{The published rules.} Under the published rules the filter is not","+node-sound~\\leanref{notsound}. In a check of 570,206 runs with memo and old","+move, it lost the last solution at a node in 58 runs, all on graphs built","+around Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} and all at $k$ equal to the optimum.","+Every one found a solution through another branch and answered correctly. No","+whole instance on which the published rules answer \\emph{unsat} wrongly is","+known."," ","-\\begin{table}[t]","-\\centering\\small","-\\caption{The customer search in the vocabulary of exact pathwidth algorithms.\\ Appendix~\\ref{app:lean} gives the formal names.}","-\\label{tab:dictionary}","-\\begin{tabularx}{\\linewidth}{@{}>{\\raggedright}p{5.2cm}>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X@{}}","-\\toprule","-Customer search & Layout \\\\","-\\midrule","-closing order from $\\emptyset$ & vertex sequence, read forwards \\\\","-open stacks $b(T)$ & border size $d(T)$ \\\\","-$\\cost(T,c)$ & $d(T\\cup\\{c\\})+1$ \\\\","-$\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)$ & $\\vs(G)\\le k-1$ \\\\","-free closure $\\cl(T)$ & full set of $T$ \\citep[p.~328]{SuchanVillanger2009} \\\\","-memo & failure table \\\\","-definite move, repaired & commitment to the child \\\\","-\\bottomrule\\end{tabularx}","-\\end{table}","+\\subsection{The rules in pathwidth language}","+\\label{sec:layouts}"," ","-\\citet[Algorithm~1, p.~393]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014} decide whether a vertex","-set extends to a $w$-feasible permutation: replace the set by a committed","-extension, answer from a failure table or at $V$, recurse on each feasible","-one-vertex extension, record failures. The customer search is this loop with","-$w=k-1$: the free closure and the definite move play the part of the commitment,","-the memo is the failure table, and the cost cut is the feasibility test.","-\\citet[\\S3.2]{CoudertMazauricNisse2014} has the same three parts in a branch and","-bound. Neither line cites the other in the papers we hold: \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}","-predates the commitments and both 2014 papers, and none of the pathwidth papers","-cites it.","+The definite move has an exact counterpart in the pathwidth literature, and","+this subsection makes the correspondence precise.","+\\citet[p.~142]{Kitsunai2016}, stating Tamaki's notion, call an extension $\\tau$","+of a vertex sequence $\\sigma$ \\emph{committable} when","+$|\\partial X'|\\ge|\\partial V(\\tau)|$ for every $X'$ with","+$V(\\sigma)\\subseteq X'\\subseteq V(\\tau)$. Their Lemma~1 says that if $\\sigma$","+extends to a feasible permutation, so does $\\tau$."," ","-\\paragraph{Commitments.} \\citet[p.~142]{Kitsunai2016}, stating Tamaki's notion,","-call an extension $\\tau$ of $\\sigma$ \\emph{committable} when $d(X')\\ge d(V(\\tau))$","-for every $X'$ with $V(\\sigma)\\subseteq X'\\subseteq V(\\tau)$; their Lemma~1 says","-that if $\\sigma$ extends to a feasible permutation, so does $\\tau$.","-"," \\begin{lemma}\\label{lem:endpoint}"," For $q\\notin S$ and $X=\\cl(S\\cup\\{q\\})$: the published premise holds iff","-$d(X)\\le d(S)$, the commitment condition at the single set $X'=S$; and the","+$b(X)\\le b(S)$, the commitment condition at the single set $X'=S$; and the"," repaired premise holds iff $X$ is a commitment from $S$, the condition at every"," $X'$ between them~\\leanref{endpoint}."," \\end{lemma}"," "," So Theorem~\\ref{thm:repaired} is the commitment lemma at the child, and the","-published Theorem~1 is a commitment whose interior condition was dropped. In","-Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} the endpoint test passes with $d(X)=3=d(S)$,","-while the interior set $\\{2,3,4\\}$ has border 2~\\leanref{cexnotcommit}.","-The published premise is not wasted: by \\citet[Lemma~10, Cor.~2,","-pp.~148--149]{Kitsunai2016} it guarantees a commitment to the least-border set","-between $S$ and $X$, which need not contain $q$~\\leanref{existscommit}.","-In the counterexample that set is $\\{2,3,4\\}$~\\leanref{commit234}.","-Committing there when the matching fails is a second repair that gives up no","-pruning; we have not built it.","+published definite move is a commitment whose interior condition was dropped.","+In Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} the endpoint test passes with","+$b(X)=3=b(S)$, while the interior set $\\{2,3,4\\}$ has border","+2~\\leanref{cexnotcommit}. The published premise is not wasted: by","+\\citet[Lemma~10, Cor.~2, pp.~148--149]{Kitsunai2016} it guarantees a","+commitment to the least-border set between $S$ and $X$, which need not contain","+$q$~\\leanref{existscommit}. In the counterexample that set is","+$\\{2,3,4\\}$~\\leanref{commit234}. Committing there when the matching fails is a","+second repair that gives up no pruning; we have not built it."," ","-A commitment of depth one, $S$ to $S\\cup\\{q\\}$, holds iff $\\open(q,S)\\le1$~\\leanref{depth1}.","-The greedy step of \\citet[Lemma~3, p.~49]{CoudertMazauricNisse2014} is exactly","-that condition~\\leanref{greedy}, and the push rule of","+A commitment of depth one, $S$ to $S\\cup\\{q\\}$, holds iff","+$\\open(q,S)\\le1$~\\leanref{depth1}. The greedy step of","+\\citet[Lemma~3, p.~49]{CoudertMazauricNisse2014} is exactly that","+condition~\\leanref{greedy}, and the push rule of"," \\citet[Rule~1, p.~330]{SuchanVillanger2009} is a case of it. So every published"," pathwidth rule of this kind lies in the region $\\open\\le1$, where the published","-definite move is right; \\citet[p.~388, Table~2]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014} report","-depth-one commitments as ``extremely effective''. Chu and Stuckey's Theorem~1","-reaches into $\\open\\ge2$, and that is where it fails. For the cost of the test,","-\\citet[Cor.~2]{Kitsunai2016} find the least border between the ends of a given","-extension by a minimum $s$--$t$ separator; Theorem~\\ref{thm:matching} is that","-computation for the single target $X$, where it becomes a bipartite matching.","+definite move is right; \\citet[p.~388, Table~2]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014}","+report depth-one commitments as ``extremely effective''. Chu and Stuckey's","+definite move reaches into $\\open\\ge2$, and that is where it fails. For the","+cost of the test, \\citet[Cor.~2]{Kitsunai2016} find the least border between","+the ends of a given extension by a minimum $s$--$t$ separator;","+Theorem~\\ref{thm:matching} is that computation for the single target $X$, where","+it becomes a bipartite matching."," ","-Table~\\ref{tab:known} sums up what is known, limited to the pathwidth papers we","-hold \\citep{SuchanVillanger2009,Bodlaender2012,CoudertMazauricNisse2014,CoudertMazauricNisse2016,KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014,Kitsunai2016}.","-``None found'' is not a claim of priority.","+Table~\\ref{tab:known} sums up, and its rows are grouped by what they say. The","+published definite and better moves are false. The free move and the repaired","+definite move are known pathwidth results. The subset rule, the repaired","+better move and the old move have no counterpart we found, and are proved","+here, as is the matching test. The comparison is limited to the pathwidth","+papers we hold","+\\citep{SuchanVillanger2009,Bodlaender2012,CoudertMazauricNisse2014,CoudertMazauricNisse2016,KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014,Kitsunai2016};","+``none found'' is not a claim of priority."," "," \\begin{table}[t]"," \\centering\\small","-\\caption{Each rule of the customer search against the exact-pathwidth literature.}","+\\caption{Two published rules are false, two repaired or reused rules are known","+pathwidth results, and the rest, with the matching test, are proved here: each","+rule of the customer search against the exact-pathwidth literature.}"," \\label{tab:known}"," \\begin{tabularx}{\\linewidth}{@{}>{\\raggedright}p{3.2cm}>{\\raggedright}X>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}p{4.1cm}@{}}"," \\toprule"," Rule & Published counterpart & Status \\\\"," \\midrule","+definite move, published & -- & false (Cex.~\\ref{cex:definite}) \\\\","+better move, published & none found & false \\\\","+\\midrule"," free move & full sets \\citep{SuchanVillanger2009}; \\citet[Prop.~3]{Kitsunai2016} & known \\\\"," definite move, $\\open\\le1$ & depth-one commitments; greedy step; push rule & known \\\\","-definite move, published & -- & false (Cex.~\\ref{cex:definite}) \\\\"," definite move, repaired & commitment lemma \\citep[Lemma~1]{Kitsunai2016} & known; matching test (Thm~\\ref{thm:matching}) not found \\\\","+memo & subset DP \\citep{Bodlaender2012}; prefix and failure tables & known; with the old move, proved here (Thm~\\ref{thm:runs}) \\\\","+\\midrule"," subset rule & none found & proved here \\\\","-better move, published & none found & false \\\\"," better move, repaired & none found & proved here \\\\"," old move & none found & proved here \\\\","-memo & subset DP \\citep{Bodlaender2012}; prefix and failure tables & known; with the old move, proved here (Thm~\\ref{thm:runs}) \\\\"," \\bottomrule"," \\end{tabularx}"," \\end{table}"," ","-\\paragraph{Prior reports.} We looked for any earlier report of the error. We","-listed the works citing \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}, Chu's thesis and its IJCAI 2013","-abstract in OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar and OpenCitations: 141 works. Of the 38","-that cite the CP paper we read 24 in full (63\\%), and of the 103 that cite only","-the thesis, 32 (31\\%). No","-work read gives a counterexample, an erratum or a correction. One,","-\\citet{Fink2012}, restates the definite move as true with a stronger premise,","-refuted above. Three use an implementation of the rules and report no failure;","-among them \\citet{Frinhani2018} ran the original code to obtain the optima that","-\\citet{MartinYanassePinto2022} reuse. We found no prior report of the error.","-The pathwidth literature has the correct form of the rule, as a commitment, and","-none of the pathwidth papers we hold mentions MOSP.","-","+\\paragraph{Prior reports.} We found no earlier report of the error, in 56 of","+the 141 works that cite the customer search, read in full","+(Appendix~\\ref{app:prior}). The pathwidth literature has the correct form of","+the rule, as a commitment, and none of the pathwidth papers we hold mentions","+MOSP."]}],"originalFile":"% Section 4. Sources: paper2/revised_algorithm.md (4.1-4.5), search_soundness.md,\n% prior_art_counterexample.md (prior art). Lean: lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/,\n% names in Appendix A (sec9a_appendix_lean.tex). The old 4.6-4.7 are Section 5.\n% Page numbers for Chu & Stuckey (2009) are those of the preprint\n% literature/chu_stuckey_2009.pdf; for Chu (2011) the printed ones.\n\\section{An exact open-stacks search, read as a pathwidth solver}\n\\label{sec:search}\n\nThe best exact method for MOSP on the standard benchmarks is the \\emph{customer\nsearch} of \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}. It searches over the orders in which the\ncustomers' stacks close, not over product sequences, and prunes with dominance\nrules, three of them stated as theorems (their Theorems~1, 2 and~3) and a fourth,\nthe subset rule, in their \\S2. \\citet{ChuStuckey2009} place MOSP among ``graph\npath-width and gate matrix layout'', citing \\citet{LY2002}, but do not use\npathwidth in the search. By Theorem~\\ref{thm:mosp} it is nevertheless a\npathwidth algorithm: any graph is the MOSP graph of the instance with one\ncustomer per vertex and one product per edge, whose optimum is pathwidth plus\none.\n\nThis section reads the search that way, and finds three things. Two of the\npublished theorems are false, and so are their restatements in Chu's thesis\n\\citep{Chu2011} and, with a stronger premise, in \\citet{Fink2012}. The repaired\nfirst rule is an instance of a known pathwidth theorem, Tamaki's commitment\nlemma; the published rule is that lemma with its interior condition dropped. And\nthe whole repaired search is proved sound in Lean.\n\n\\subsection{The search decides pathwidth}\n\\label{sec:search-def}\n\n\\citet[p.~4]{ChuStuckey2009} observe that the products matter only through the\nMOSP graph. So fix a graph $G=(C,E)$ on the customers and an integer $k\\ge0$.\nFor a customer $c$ and a set $T\\subseteq C$ of closed customers, write\n$N[c]$ for the closed neighbourhood of $c$, and\n\\begin{align*}\nO(T) &= \\textstyle\\bigcup_{c\\in T}N[c] && \\text{the stacks opened once $T$ has closed},\\\\\n\\fin(X) &= \\{c : N[c]\\subseteq X\\} && \\text{the customers free to close when $X$ is open},\\\\\n\\cl(T) &= \\fin(O(T)) && \\text{the \\emph{free closure} of $T$},\\\\\nb(T) &= |O(T)\\setminus T| && \\text{the stacks open after $T$ has closed},\\\\\no(c,T) &= N[c]\\setminus O(T),\\quad \\open(c,T)=|o(c,T)| && \\text{the stacks closing $c$ would newly open.}\n\\end{align*}\nA \\emph{move} at $T$ closes one customer $c\\notin T$, at cost\n$\\cost(T,c)=|O(T\\cup\\{c\\})\\setminus T| = b(T\\cup\\{c\\})+1$, the stacks open at\nthat moment counting $c$; it is \\emph{playable} when $\\cost(T,c)\\le k$. Write\n$P_k(T)$ when some order of $C\\setminus T$ has every step playable. On entering\na node the search closes every free customer, so it stands only at\n\\emph{states}, sets with $S=\\cl(S)$, and the \\emph{child} of $S$ by $c$ is\n$S\\cdot c=\\cl(S\\cup\\{c\\})$. The search's predicate $\\Sol_k(S)$ holds when $S=C$,\nor when some playable $c$ has $\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$~\\leanref{defs}.\nEvery node the search visits satisfies the invariant $b(S)\\le k$.\n\n\\begin{lemma}[free moves never hurt]\\label{lem:F}\nIf $b(T)\\le k$, then $P_k(T)\\iff\\Sol_k(\\cl(T))$~\\leanref{F}.\n\\end{lemma}\nClosing a customer whose neighbourhood is open opens nothing, never makes a\nlater step dearer, and leaves one fewer stack open. The hypothesis is needed:\nclosing the free customers one by one costs $b(T)$ at each step.\n\n\\begin{theorem}[the search decides pathwidth]\\label{thm:decides}\nFor nonempty $C$, $\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)\\iff\\pw(G)+1\\le k$; on the MOSP graph of an\ninstance $M$ with a requirement, $\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)\\iff Z(M)\\le k$~\\leanref{decides}.\n\\end{theorem}\n\\begin{proof}\nFor a closing order $c_1,\\dots,c_n$ with prefixes $S_i$, the $i$-th step costs\n$1+|\\partial S_i|$, where $\\partial X$ is the set of vertices outside $X$ with a\nneighbour in $X$. Read backwards as a layout, the cut separating $C\\setminus S_i$\nfrom $S_i$ counts exactly $\\partial S_i$. So the cost of the order is the vertex\nseparation of the reversed layout plus one, and Lemma~\\ref{lem:F} at the root and\nTheorem~\\ref{thm:vs} finish the proof. The Lean statement goes through\nnarrowness \\citep{LY2002ref11}, which counts the forward order directly.\n\\end{proof}\n\nSo a refutation, $\\neg\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)$, may only mean $\\pw(G)>k-1$. The rest\nof the section asks whether the pruning rules preserve that meaning.\n\n\\paragraph{What a rule must satisfy.} At a state $S$ the search has a set $Q$ of\n\\emph{old moves} (Section~\\ref{sec:other-rules}). Its candidates are the\ncustomers outside $S\\cup Q$; the \\emph{cost cut} keeps the playable ones, $P$,\nin index order, and a \\emph{filter} keeps a sublist $L\\subseteq P$. The filter\nis \\emph{node-sound} at $(S,Q)$ if, whenever every $q\\in Q$ has\n$\\neg\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$, $\\Sol_k(S)$ implies $\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$ for some $c\\in L$~\\leanref{nodesound}.\nEach rule justifies a discarded move $r$ by a \\emph{covering}, a move $c$ with\n$\\Sol_k(S\\cdot r)\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$. A node is sound when every link a\nrule cites is a true covering and every chain of links from a discarded\ncandidate ends in $L$ or in $Q$; the second condition can fail through a cycle\neven when every link is true. The filter runs the definite move, then the subset\nrule, then the better move, each once.\n\n\\subsection{The definite move is false as published}\n\\label{sec:definite}\n\n\\citet[\\S3.1, p.~6]{ChuStuckey2009} state:\n\\begin{quote}\n\\textbf{Theorem 1.} Suppose S ++ [q] is playable and close(q, S) $\\ge$\nopen(q, S), then if U$'$ = S ++ R is a solution, there exists a solution\nU = S ++ [q] ++ R$'$,\n\\end{quote}\nwhere $\\close(q,S)$ counts the customers $d$ with $o(d,S)\\subseteq o(q,S)$\n(p.~4). The rule keeps $q$ alone. We read $d$ as ranging over customers not yet\nclosed, as the code does; read literally, closed customers count too, which\nweakens the premise, and both readings are refuted below. \\citet[Thm~6.3.6,\np.~142]{Chu2011} restates the theorem with the same premise and proof, reworded\nfor $k$ stacks. Write $X=S\\cdot q$.\n\n\\begin{lemma}[what the premise says]\\label{lem:premise}\n$\\close(q,S)=|X\\setminus S|$. Hence the premise holds iff $b(X)\\le b(S)$: the\nchild has no more open stacks than the parent~\\leanref{premise}.\n\\end{lemma}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.85\\linewidth]{definite_counterexample.pdf}\n\\caption{The graph of Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} at the state where the\ndefinite move fails. Customer 2 is closed; 1, 3 and 4 are open. Moving 0 opens\nthe new stacks 0, 7 and 11 and closes 0, 3 and 4, so open = close = 3 and the\nrule keeps 0 alone. But 3 and 4 have the single new stack 0 between them:\nclosing them first gains two stacks for the price of one, which a solution\nstarting with 0 cannot match.}\n\\label{fig:cex}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{counterexample}\\label{cex:definite}\nLet $G$ be the graph on customers $0,\\dots,13$ with the 26 edges\n\\begin{gather*}\n0\\text{--}3,\\ 0\\text{--}4,\\ 0\\text{--}7,\\ 0\\text{--}11,\\ 1\\text{--}2,\\ 1\\text{--}5,\\ 1\\text{--}6,\\ 2\\text{--}3,\\ 2\\text{--}4,\\ 5\\text{--}9,\\ 5\\text{--}11,\\ 5\\text{--}12,\\ 6\\text{--}8,\\\\\n6\\text{--}13,\\ 7\\text{--}9,\\ 7\\text{--}10,\\ 7\\text{--}13,\\ 8\\text{--}9,\\ 8\\text{--}10,\\ 8\\text{--}11,\\ 9\\text{--}10,\\ 9\\text{--}11,\\ 10\\text{--}11,\\ 10\\text{--}12,\\ 10\\text{--}13,\\ 12\\text{--}13,\n\\end{gather*}\nwith $S=\\{2\\}$, $k=6$ and $q=0$ (Figure~\\ref{fig:cex}). Then $O(S)=\\{1,2,3,4\\}$,\n$o(0,S)=\\{0,7,11\\}$ and $\\cost(S,0)=6$, so $0$ is playable; the customers with\n$o(d,S)\\subseteq\\{0,7,11\\}$ are $0$, $3$ and $4$, so $\\close(0,S)=3\\ge\\open(0,S)$;\nand $0$ comes first in index order, so the rule keeps only $0$. A solution\nexists from $S$: the order\n\\[1,3,4,6,12,13,0,5,7,8,9,10,11\\]\ncosts at most 6. None exists\nfrom the child $X=\\{0,2,3,4\\}$: only seven sets are reachable from $X$ by moves\nof cost at most 6, and none is $C$~\\leanref{cex}.\n\\end{counterexample}\n\nThe published implication, as a universal statement, is refuted in Lean under\nboth readings of $\\close$, and so is the thesis's Theorem~6.3.6~\\leanref{thm1false}.\nAppendix~\\ref{app:minimality} gives the search for smaller counterexamples, and\nAppendix~\\ref{app:whyfails} the analysis of why the published proof fails.\n\\citet[pp.~27--28]{Fink2012} restates the rule with a\nstronger premise, counting only dominated customers of smaller index than $q$.\nThe graph above refutes it under no labelling, but a third twin of 3 and 4 gives\na 15-customer graph that does~\\leanref{fink}.\n\n\\paragraph{The repair.} Call $q$ \\emph{hereditarily definite} at $S$ when\n$b(X)\\le b(B)$ for every $B$ with $S\\subseteq B\\subseteq X$ and $q\\notin B$. By\nLemma~\\ref{lem:premise}, the published premise is the case $B=S$. In the\ncounterexample the repair fails at $B=\\{2,3,4\\}$, where $b(B)=2<3=b(X)$~\\leanref{hered}.\n\n\\begin{lemma}[open stacks are submodular]\\label{lem:submod}\nFor all $A,B\\subseteq C$, $b(A\\cup B)+b(A\\cap B)\\le b(A)+b(B)$~\\leanref{submod}.\n\\end{lemma}\n\\begin{proof}\n$b(T)=|O(T)|-|T|$, $O(A\\cup B)=O(A)\\cup O(B)$ and $O(A\\cap B)\\subseteq\nO(A)\\cap O(B)$; the customers themselves balance exactly.\n\\end{proof}\n\n\\begin{theorem}[the repaired definite move is sound]\\label{thm:repaired}\nIf $q\\notin S$ is hereditarily definite at $S$, then $P_k(S)\\Rightarrow P_k(X)$.\nAt a state with the invariant, $\\Sol_k(S)\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$~\\leanref{repaired}.\n\\end{theorem}\n\\begin{proof}\nTake an order from $S$ of cost at most $k$ with prefixes $T_i$, in which $q$ is\nthe $j$-th step, and follow it from $X$, skipping customers already in $X$. A\nstep $i<j$ closes $c_i$ at $T_{i-1}\\cup X$. With $Z=T_i$ we have\n$S\\subseteq Z\\cap X\\subseteq X$ and $q\\notin Z$, so the hypothesis gives\n$b(X)\\le b(Z\\cap X)$, and Lemma~\\ref{lem:submod} gives $b(Z\\cup X)\\le b(Z)$: the\nstep costs no more than the original. After step $j-1$ the closed set contains\n$T_j$ with the same opened set, and the rest of the order applies. The search's\nform follows by Lemma~\\ref{lem:F}.\n\\end{proof}\n\nThis theorem is not new. The hereditary premise is the commitment condition of\nTamaki, and the proof is the proof of the commitment lemma,\n\\citet[Lemma~1, p.~142]{Kitsunai2016}; Section~\\ref{sec:layouts} makes the\ncorrespondence exact. What we did not find in the papers we hold is the\nfollowing cheap form of the test.\n\n\\begin{theorem}[the repair is a matching condition]\\label{thm:matching}\nLet $Y=X\\setminus(S\\cup\\{q\\})$, and join each $d\\in Y$ to the stacks of\n$o(d,S)\\subseteq o(q,S)$. Then $q$ is hereditarily definite at $S$ iff this\nbipartite graph has a matching with at least $\\open(q,S)-1$ edges~\\leanref{matching}.\n\\end{theorem}\n\\begin{proof}\nThe sets of the repair are $B=S\\cup D$, $D\\subseteq Y$, and\n$b(X)\\le b(S\\cup D)$ iff $|D|-|o(D)|\\le\\delta$ with\n$\\delta=|Y|+1-\\open(q,S)$, where $o(D)$ is the union of the $o(d,S)$. By the\ndeficiency form of Hall's theorem the largest matching has size\n$|Y|-\\max_D(|D|-|o(D)|)$, which is at least $|Y|-\\delta=\\open(q,S)-1$ exactly\nwhen the repair holds. The Lean proof reduces the deficiency form to Mathlib's\nHall theorem with $\\delta$ dummy stacks.\n\\end{proof}\n\nIn words: the rule is safe exactly when all but one of the new stacks can be\npaired with a different freed customer who needed it. In the counterexample,\nclosing 0 opens three new stacks and frees 3 and 4, both of whom needed only\nstack 0: one pairing where two are required. Every $q$ with $\\open(q,S)\\le1$\npasses at once, so the published rule is right there~\\leanref{open1}.\n\n\\subsection{The other rules}\n\\label{sec:other-rules}\n\n\\paragraph{The subset rule.} ``If $o(c_i,S)\\subseteq o(c_j,S)$ and $i<j$ \\dots\\\nmove $j$ can be removed'' \\citep[\\S2, p.~5]{ChuStuckey2009}. As coded, $d$\ndominates $r$ when $o(d,S)\\subseteq o(r,S)$ and the inclusion is strict or $d<r$;\nevery dominated playable $r$ is dropped, with a fallback that keeps $P$ if all\nwould go. The covering is sound for every set $S$: if $o(d,S)\\subseteq o(r,S)$\nand $r$ is playable, then $d$ is playable and $\\Sol_k(S\\cdot r)\\Rightarrow\n\\Sol_k(S\\cdot d)$, because $d$ is free after $r$ and closing $d$ then $r$ reaches\nthe same state at no greater cost. With the index tie-break, domination is a\nstrict order, so an undominated move with a solution survives, and the fallback\nnever fires when a solution exists~\\leanref{subset}.\nThe tie-break is necessary: without it, two customers with equal new stacks\ndominate each other and both go, which loses a solution on a 7-customer\ngraph~\\leanref{tiebreak}.\n\n\\paragraph{The better move.} \\citet[\\S3.2, p.~6]{ChuStuckey2009}: ``Suppose\nS ++ [q] and S ++ [r, q] are playable and close(q, S $\\cup$ \\{r\\}) $\\ge$\nopen(q, S $\\cup$ \\{r\\}) then if U$'$ = S ++ [r] ++ R is a solution there exists a\nsolution U = S ++ [q] ++ R$'$.'' Its premise is Theorem~1's premise for $q$ at the\nchild $\\cl(S\\cup\\{r\\})$, together with playability of $r$ then $q$, and its\nconclusion swaps $q$ and $r$~\\leanref{isbetter}. The swap is sound; the appeal to\nTheorem~1 is not. On the graph of Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} at the root\nwith $k=6$, the definite move does not fire, customers 0 and 2 both survive the\nsubset rule, and the premise holds for $r=2$, $q=0$, so the rule drops 2 citing\n0. But $\\{2\\}$ has a solution and $\\{0\\}$ has none~\\leanref{bettercex}.\nAt that node another kept customer still has a solution, so this is a false link\nand not a lost node; no node where the better move loses the last solution has\nbeen found. \\citet[Thm~6.3.8, p.~143]{Chu2011} states the better move with a\ndifferent premise, $\\close(q,S)\\ge\\open(q,S\\cup\\{r\\})$, and proves it through\nTheorem~6.3.6. The same graph refutes it at $S=\\{2\\}$, $r=3$, $q=0$~\\leanref{thm638}.\nThe repair cites $q$ for $r$ only if $q$ is hereditarily definite at\n$\\cl(S\\cup\\{r\\})$, one matching at the child; then the swap argument goes\nthrough~\\leanref{repbetter}.\n\nTwo earlier faults of this rule in our own implementation are now proved in\nLean too: a close count that included the customers $r$ finishes on its own,\nand an order of the rules in which the subset rule could cite a customer the\nbetter move had already dropped, which loses a node through a cycle of true\nlinks~\\leanref{bugs}.\nThe second is an instance of the general observation of\n\\citet[Prop.~3, Example~6]{Beck2025} that mutually dominating transitions must\nbe tie-broken.\n\n\\paragraph{The old move.} \\citet[Thm~3, p.~7]{ChuStuckey2009} let a child inherit\na sibling $q$ whose subtree failed, provided that inserting $q$ before each\nlater step keeps that step playable. The rule is sound as coded: if\n$\\cost(S\\cup\\{q\\},c)\\le k$, then\n$\\Sol_k(\\cl(\\{q\\}\\cup S\\cdot c))\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$, so a refuted\nsibling stays refuted along any path whose steps pass the test~\\leanref{oldmove}.\nThe test is necessary; the smallest graph that shows it is the path on five\nvertices~\\leanref{reinsert}.\n\n\\paragraph{The memo.} A refuted state is recorded and refused when met again.\nWhether a state with $b(S)\\le k$ has a solution depends on the state alone, not\non the path that reached it~\\leanref{memo}. One implementation of\nours declined to run the memo beside the old move, on the ground that a\nrefutation obtained with inherited old moves depends on the ancestors. A node\ntaken on its own, with an old move that is not in fact refuted, can indeed\nrecord a solvable state~\\leanref{fakeold}; Theorem~\\ref{thm:runs} (Appendix~\\ref{app:machinery}) shows that this\nnever happens in a real run.\n\n\\subsection{The repaired search is sound}\n\\label{sec:theorem}\n\nThe main theorem rests on two lemmas about the filter and about whole runs,\nProposition~\\ref{prop:filter} and Theorem~\\ref{thm:runs}, which\nAppendix~\\ref{app:machinery} states and proves.\n\n\\begin{theorem}[the revised search is sound]\\label{thm:main}\nRun the customer search with free moves, the memo, old moves and the filter\ndefinite $\\to$ subset $\\to$ better, the definite move firing only on a\nhereditarily definite customer and the better move citing only under the\nrepaired premise. If it refutes $k$ from the root, then no closing order costs at\nmost $k$, $\\pw(G)>k-1$, and on the MOSP graph of an instance $M$ with a\nrequirement, $Z(M)>k$~\\leanref{main}.\n\\end{theorem}\n\nWhat the Lean model covers is the search as specified; that our C and Python\nimplement it is checked, not proved. A transcription of the Lean filter agreed\nwith the production filter on 16,244,090 node checks over graphs on 1 to 17\nvertices; the matching agreed with the hereditary premise on 36,939,226\ncandidates; and a search written from the theorems agreed with the C, the Python\nand an exact oracle on 17,431,232 runs over 52,592 graphs on 1 to 17\nvertices. Under the published rules the filter is not\nnode-sound~\\leanref{notsound}, and in a check of 570,206 runs with\nmemo and old move it lost the last solution at a node in 58 runs, all on graphs\nbuilt around Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} and all at $k$ equal to the\noptimum. Every one found a solution through another branch and answered\ncorrectly. No whole instance on which the published rules answer \\emph{unsat}\nwrongly is known.\n\n\\subsection{The search in pathwidth language}\n\\label{sec:layouts}\n\nFor a set $T$ of vertices write $N(T)$ for its \\emph{border}, the vertices\noutside $T$ with a neighbour inside, and $d(T)=|N(T)|$, in the notation of\n\\citet[p.~390]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014}. A vertex sequence is $w$-feasible when\nevery prefix has $d\\le w$, and $\\vs(G)$ is the least $w$ for which some\npermutation is $w$-feasible. Table~\\ref{tab:dictionary} translates.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{The customer search in the vocabulary of exact pathwidth algorithms.\\ Appendix~\\ref{app:lean} gives the formal names.}\n\\label{tab:dictionary}\n\\begin{tabularx}{\\linewidth}{@{}>{\\raggedright}p{5.2cm}>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X@{}}\n\\toprule\nCustomer search & Layout \\\\\n\\midrule\nclosing order from $\\emptyset$ & vertex sequence, read forwards \\\\\nopen stacks $b(T)$ & border size $d(T)$ \\\\\n$\\cost(T,c)$ & $d(T\\cup\\{c\\})+1$ \\\\\n$\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)$ & $\\vs(G)\\le k-1$ \\\\\nfree closure $\\cl(T)$ & full set of $T$ \\citep[p.~328]{SuchanVillanger2009} \\\\\nmemo & failure table \\\\\ndefinite move, repaired & commitment to the child \\\\\n\\bottomrule\\end{tabularx}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\citet[Algorithm~1, p.~393]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014} decide whether a vertex\nset extends to a $w$-feasible permutation: replace the set by a committed\nextension, answer from a failure table or at $V$, recurse on each feasible\none-vertex extension, record failures. The customer search is this loop with\n$w=k-1$: the free closure and the definite move play the part of the commitment,\nthe memo is the failure table, and the cost cut is the feasibility test.\n\\citet[\\S3.2]{CoudertMazauricNisse2014} has the same three parts in a branch and\nbound. Neither line cites the other in the papers we hold: \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}\npredates the commitments and both 2014 papers, and none of the pathwidth papers\ncites it.\n\n\\paragraph{Commitments.} \\citet[p.~142]{Kitsunai2016}, stating Tamaki's notion,\ncall an extension $\\tau$ of $\\sigma$ \\emph{committable} when $d(X')\\ge d(V(\\tau))$\nfor every $X'$ with $V(\\sigma)\\subseteq X'\\subseteq V(\\tau)$; their Lemma~1 says\nthat if $\\sigma$ extends to a feasible permutation, so does $\\tau$.\n\n\\begin{lemma}\\label{lem:endpoint}\nFor $q\\notin S$ and $X=\\cl(S\\cup\\{q\\})$: the published premise holds iff\n$d(X)\\le d(S)$, the commitment condition at the single set $X'=S$; and the\nrepaired premise holds iff $X$ is a commitment from $S$, the condition at every\n$X'$ between them~\\leanref{endpoint}.\n\\end{lemma}\n\nSo Theorem~\\ref{thm:repaired} is the commitment lemma at the child, and the\npublished Theorem~1 is a commitment whose interior condition was dropped. In\nCounterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} the endpoint test passes with $d(X)=3=d(S)$,\nwhile the interior set $\\{2,3,4\\}$ has border 2~\\leanref{cexnotcommit}.\nThe published premise is not wasted: by \\citet[Lemma~10, Cor.~2,\npp.~148--149]{Kitsunai2016} it guarantees a commitment to the least-border set\nbetween $S$ and $X$, which need not contain $q$~\\leanref{existscommit}.\nIn the counterexample that set is $\\{2,3,4\\}$~\\leanref{commit234}.\nCommitting there when the matching fails is a second repair that gives up no\npruning; we have not built it.\n\nA commitment of depth one, $S$ to $S\\cup\\{q\\}$, holds iff $\\open(q,S)\\le1$~\\leanref{depth1}.\nThe greedy step of \\citet[Lemma~3, p.~49]{CoudertMazauricNisse2014} is exactly\nthat condition~\\leanref{greedy}, and the push rule of\n\\citet[Rule~1, p.~330]{SuchanVillanger2009} is a case of it. So every published\npathwidth rule of this kind lies in the region $\\open\\le1$, where the published\ndefinite move is right; \\citet[p.~388, Table~2]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014} report\ndepth-one commitments as ``extremely effective''. Chu and Stuckey's Theorem~1\nreaches into $\\open\\ge2$, and that is where it fails. For the cost of the test,\n\\citet[Cor.~2]{Kitsunai2016} find the least border between the ends of a given\nextension by a minimum $s$--$t$ separator; Theorem~\\ref{thm:matching} is that\ncomputation for the single target $X$, where it becomes a bipartite matching.\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:known} sums up what is known, limited to the pathwidth papers we\nhold \\citep{SuchanVillanger2009,Bodlaender2012,CoudertMazauricNisse2014,CoudertMazauricNisse2016,KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014,Kitsunai2016}.\n``None found'' is not a claim of priority.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{Each rule of the customer search against the exact-pathwidth literature.}\n\\label{tab:known}\n\\begin{tabularx}{\\linewidth}{@{}>{\\raggedright}p{3.2cm}>{\\raggedright}X>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}p{4.1cm}@{}}\n\\toprule\nRule & Published counterpart & Status \\\\\n\\midrule\nfree move & full sets \\citep{SuchanVillanger2009}; \\citet[Prop.~3]{Kitsunai2016} & known \\\\\ndefinite move, $\\open\\le1$ & depth-one commitments; greedy step; push rule & known \\\\\ndefinite move, published & -- & false (Cex.~\\ref{cex:definite}) \\\\\ndefinite move, repaired & commitment lemma \\citep[Lemma~1]{Kitsunai2016} & known; matching test (Thm~\\ref{thm:matching}) not found \\\\\nsubset rule & none found & proved here \\\\\nbetter move, published & none found & false \\\\\nbetter move, repaired & none found & proved here \\\\\nold move & none found & proved here \\\\\nmemo & subset DP \\citep{Bodlaender2012}; prefix and failure tables & known; with the old move, proved here (Thm~\\ref{thm:runs}) \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabularx}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Prior reports.} We looked for any earlier report of the error. We\nlisted the works citing \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}, Chu's thesis and its IJCAI 2013\nabstract in OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar and OpenCitations: 141 works. Of the 38\nthat cite the CP paper we read 24 in full (63\\%), and of the 103 that cite only\nthe thesis, 32 (31\\%). No\nwork read gives a counterexample, an erratum or a correction. One,\n\\citet{Fink2012}, restates the definite move as true with a stronger premise,\nrefuted above. Three use an implementation of the rules and report no failure;\namong them \\citet{Frinhani2018} ran the original code to obtain the optima that\n\\citet{MartinYanassePinto2022} reuse. We found no prior report of the error.\nThe pathwidth literature has the correct form of the rule, as a commitment, and\nnone of the pathwidth papers we hold mentions MOSP.\n\n","userModified":false}}
