{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YLqNbDXhcPbyZy7j9n6Wzk","type":"tool_result","content":"== 12\nproves all 50 VSPLIB trees, against their 30, and 39 of the 73 Harwell–Boeing graphs, against their 26.\nThese are comparisons with published numbers obtained on other hardware, not a head-to-head run.\nTwo consequences shape the rest of the paper. First, a result on any of the twelve problems should be\n== 13\nTable 4.1: The customer search is, ingredient by ingredient, an exact pathwidth algorithm: each notion of the search\nbeside its name in the pathwidth literature. The memo and the definite move are described in Section 4.2.\nCustomer search\n== 14\nAlgorithm 4.1 The customer search (Chu and Stuckey, 2009). It decides Solk (S) for a graph G = (C, E) and\na stack bound k, and is called as S EARCH( /0, /0). Q is the set of old moves the node inherits.\n1\n== 15\nA counterexample to Chu & Stuckey's Theorem 1, at k = 6\n0 is playable and close(0, S) open(0, S), so the rule keeps 0 alone. A solution from S exists\n(first moves 1, 3, 4, 6), but none starts with 0.\n== 16\nwith S = {2}, k = 6 and q = 0. Then O(S) = {1, 2, 3, 4}, o(0, S) = {0, 7, 11} and cost(S, 0) = 6, so 0 is\nplayable. The customers with o(d, S) ⊆ {0, 7, 11} are 0, 3 and 4, so close(0, S) = 3 ≥ open(0, S), and 0\ncomes first in index order, so the rule keeps only 0. A solution exists from S: the order\n== 17\nProof. The sets of the repair are B = S ∪ D, D ⊆ Y , and b(X) ≤ b(S ∪ D) iff |D| − |o(D)| ≤ δ with δ =\n|Y | + 1 − open(q, S), where o(D) is the union of the o(d, S). By the deficiency form of Hall’s theorem the\nlargest matching has size |Y | − maxD (|D| − |o(D)|), which is at least |Y | − δ = open(q, S) − 1 exactly when\n== 18\nThe old move. Chu and Stuckey (2009, Thm 3, p. 7) let a child inherit a sibling q whose subtree failed,\nprovided that inserting q before each later step keeps that step playable (line 9 of Algorithm 4.1). The\nrule is sound as implemented: if cost(S ∪ {q}, c) ≤ k, then Solk (cl({q} ∪ S · c)) ⇒ Solk (S · q), so a refuted\n== 19\nThe published rules. Under the published rules the filter is not node-sound (Appendix A, A.46). In a\ncheck of 570,206 runs with memo and old move, it lost the last solution at a node in 58 runs, all on graphs\nbuilt around Counterexample 4.4 and all at k equal to the optimum. Every one found a solution through\n== 20\nTable 4.2: Two published rules are false; the free move, the memo and the repaired definite move are known pathwidth\nresults; the subset rule, the repaired better move and the old move, like the matching test, are proved here. Each rule\nof the customer search against the exact-pathwidth literature.\n== 21\nTable 5.1: The cost of the repair: nodes under the published and the repaired rules, in paired runs of the same decision\nin one worker. Totals over the pairs that finished under both settings; a censored pair says nothing about cost. MOSP\nrows refute the optimum minus one; the graph row is whole descents at 120 s each.\n== 22\nTable 5.3: Certificates for the repaired rules on the certified corpus at 9–75 customers, refuting the optimum minus one,\n60 s emission budget. Unknown: emission did not finish. Below 41 customers the default rows match csearch\nand are omitted.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a1c55f7b-98f8-4ea9-815f-35ebfa760af6","uuid":"ce9cb0d2-2859-42f7-a23c-171d09598347","timestamp":"2026-10-06T16:48:14.328Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"== 12\nproves all 50 VSPLIB trees, against their 30, and 39 of the 73 Harwell–Boeing graphs, against their 26.\nThese are comparisons with published numbers obtained on other hardware, not a head-to-head run.\nTwo consequences shape the rest of the paper. First, a result on any of the twelve problems should be\n== 13\nTable 4.1: The customer search is, ingredient by ingredient, an exact pathwidth algorithm: each notion of the search\nbeside its name in the pathwidth literature. The memo and the definite move are described in Section 4.2.\nCustomer search\n== 14\nAlgorithm 4.1 The customer search (Chu and Stuckey, 2009). It decides Solk (S) for a graph G = (C, E) and\na stack bound k, and is called as S EARCH( /0, /0). Q is the set of old moves the node inherits.\n1\n== 15\nA counterexample to Chu & Stuckey's Theorem 1, at k = 6\n0 is playable and close(0, S) open(0, S), so the rule keeps 0 alone. A solution from S exists\n(first moves 1, 3, 4, 6), but none starts with 0.\n== 16\nwith S = {2}, k = 6 and q = 0. Then O(S) = {1, 2, 3, 4}, o(0, S) = {0, 7, 11} and cost(S, 0) = 6, so 0 is\nplayable. The customers with o(d, S) ⊆ {0, 7, 11} are 0, 3 and 4, so close(0, S) = 3 ≥ open(0, S), and 0\ncomes first in index order, so the rule keeps only 0. A solution exists from S: the order\n== 17\nProof. The sets of the repair are B = S ∪ D, D ⊆ Y , and b(X) ≤ b(S ∪ D) iff |D| − |o(D)| ≤ δ with δ =\n|Y | + 1 − open(q, S), where o(D) is the union of the o(d, S). By the deficiency form of Hall’s theorem the\nlargest matching has size |Y | − maxD (|D| − |o(D)|), which is at least |Y | − δ = open(q, S) − 1 exactly when\n== 18\nThe old move. Chu and Stuckey (2009, Thm 3, p. 7) let a child inherit a sibling q whose subtree failed,\nprovided that inserting q before each later step keeps that step playable (line 9 of Algorithm 4.1). The\nrule is sound as implemented: if cost(S ∪ {q}, c) ≤ k, then Solk (cl({q} ∪ S · c)) ⇒ Solk (S · q), so a refuted\n== 19\nThe published rules. Under the published rules the filter is not node-sound (Appendix A, A.46). In a\ncheck of 570,206 runs with memo and old move, it lost the last solution at a node in 58 runs, all on graphs\nbuilt around Counterexample 4.4 and all at k equal to the optimum. Every one found a solution through\n== 20\nTable 4.2: Two published rules are false; the free move, the memo and the repaired definite move are known pathwidth\nresults; the subset rule, the repaired better move and the old move, like the matching test, are proved here. Each rule\nof the customer search against the exact-pathwidth literature.\n== 21\nTable 5.1: The cost of the repair: nodes under the published and the repaired rules, in paired runs of the same decision\nin one worker. Totals over the pairs that finished under both settings; a censored pair says nothing about cost. MOSP\nrows refute the optimum minus one; the graph row is whole descents at 120 s each.\n== 22\nTable 5.3: Certificates for the repaired rules on the certified corpus at 9–75 customers, refuting the optimum minus one,\n60 s emission budget. Unknown: emission did not finish. Below 41 customers the default rows match csearch\nand are omitted.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
